/* Generated by tools/build_cycle1_page.py from reviewed instruction and source-checked templates. */
window.CYCLE1_PAGE = {"html": "<header class=\"guide-heading\"><p class=\"kicker\">Cycle 1 | Guided explanation</p><h1>Cycle 1: Analyze data to promote equity</h1></header><nav class=\"c1-contents\" aria-label=\"Cycle 1 sections\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/limits\">Submission limits</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/indicators\">Six indicators</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/equity-gap\">Equity gap</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-map\">Rubric map</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/fictional-case\">Fictional example</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-a\">Part A</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-b\">Part B</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-c\">Part C</a><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-d\">Part D</a></nav>\n<section id=\"c1-introduction\"><p>Cycle 1 asks you to investigate an equity concern at your school and develop a thoughtful response. You will examine evidence, explain what it helps you understand, and propose ways the school could better meet the needs of a particular student group.</p>\n<p>You do not need to have the explanation or solution already worked out. Your understanding should develop as you examine numerical data, gather perspectives, review school practices, and consider relevant research. An attendance pattern might initially suggest a need for more support. Further investigation might show that support already exists, but students and families encounter difficulties accessing it. Recognizing that distinction is part of the leadership work.</p>\n<p>The four parts organize this inquiry. Part A establishes the evidence and equity-gap analysis. Part B examines potential contributing factors and develops a problem statement. Part C presents proposed strategies, targeted leadership feedback, and adjustments. Part D explains what you learned about your leadership. The student group and concern should remain recognizable across all four parts.</p>\n<p>Cycle 1 concludes with an informed proposal and reflection. It does not require you to implement the strategies and demonstrate improved student outcomes. The manual explains the requirements; the templates provide the questions and response spaces. Work through the explanations below with your own school evidence and keep the original template wording.</p></section><article class=\"reading guide-reading c1-reading\"><section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you will submit</h2><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Part, Submission, Maximum length\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Submission</th><th scope=\"col\">Maximum length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Data tables and equity-gap analysis</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages of responses</strong>, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Contributing factors and problem statement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Strategies, feedback, adjustments, and partner involvement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 written pages OR 5 minutes of video explanation</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are limits for each complete part, not for each question. Submit one file per part. Written files may be DOCX, ODT, or PDF. Use the original templates with Arial 11-point type, single spacing, and one-inch margins. The original template text and your responses count toward the applicable limit, with the stated exclusion for Part A's data tables. The guide does not give charts a separate page-count exemption. Excess pages are not read or scored.</p>\n<p>First develop the evidence and reasoning each question needs; then revise for clarity and length. The teaching explanations and annotated examples on this page are longer than a candidate response. They are not a completed, page-fitted submission to copy. The Part D video option addresses the same three reflection prompts; it is not a recording of a meeting.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">CTC Cycle 1 Version 09: <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">preparing evidence, printed pp. 22-23</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">submission specifications, printed pp. 24-25</a>. Part D permits asf, qt, mov, mpg, mpeg, avi, wmv, mp4, or m4v video files.</p></section><section id=\"indicators\"><h2>Choose one of six California state indicators</h2><p>An <strong>indicator</strong> is an area of student learning or well-being that you investigate using data. CalAPA Cycle 1 requires you to select one of the following six indicators. You do not investigate all six in one submission.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Indicator, What you examine\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Indicator</th><th scope=\"col\">What you examine</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Chronic Absenteeism</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns of students missing substantial amounts of school and the resulting concern about access to learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Suspension Rate</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns of suspension, including differences among student groups.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">English Learner Progress</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">English learners' progress toward English language proficiency.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Graduation Rate</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns in students completing high school, including differences among groups.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Academic Performance</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Academic performance, including English language arts and mathematics.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">College/Career Readiness</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Students' preparation for college and careers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Begin with an indicator relevant to your school's grade levels and available evidence. Examine related quantitative data across the <strong>three most recent available years</strong>, then use that analysis to select <strong>one student group</strong> for closer investigation. If you cannot obtain three years for an indicator, the manual directs you to choose another indicator for which those data are available. Do not substitute an older convenient period without checking the requirement.</p>\n<p>An indicator and a student group are different things. Chronic Absenteeism is an indicator; students with disabilities is a possible student group. A disparity in that group's results gives you a reason to investigate. It does not tell you which school practice caused the difference.</p>\n<p>Start with official school and district records and sources such as the California School Dashboard, the Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP), Ed-Data, or the School Accountability Report Card (SARC). The <a href=\"#equity-gap-help\">Equity Gap Help</a> page shows how the separate app can assist with public-data exploration. Confirm the exact school, measure, reporting years, group definitions, counts, and denominators before using a figure.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official indicator and three-year requirements, printed pp. 6-7</a>.</p></section>\n<section id=\"equity-gap\"><h2>What an equity gap means</h2>\n<p>In plain language, an equity gap concerns differences in students' access to educational opportunities, resources, support, or outcomes that warrant examining whether the school is meeting their needs fairly. CalAPA asks you to examine a specific relationship: the resources and intended outcomes identified in school or district improvement plans, and actual performance for underserved students.</p>\n<p>This means examining <strong>school commitments, resource allocations, access to support, and outcomes together</strong>. A school may fund an attendance-support program and still need to investigate whether the intended students can use it and whether anyone checks its effectiveness. An equity-gap analysis may reveal incomplete evidence or weak monitoring as well as a difference in outcomes.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Evidence, What it helps establish\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it helps establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">A fictional group has 30% chronic absenteeism, compared with 18% schoolwide.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">A 12-percentage-point outcome difference warrants investigation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">The school plan promises accessible support and allocates staff time.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">There is a stated commitment and an allocation to examine.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Interviews, observations, and records suggest inconsistent follow-through.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">Access and monitoring may need attention; the cause of the outcome difference is not established.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Your analysis explains what the combined evidence supports and what remains uncertain. The numerical disparity alone does not establish an inequitable practice or its cause. Likewise, the existence of a program does not demonstrate that students received usable support. This is why Part A includes numerical evidence, qualitative evidence, and the school's goals.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Equity-gap analysis, printed p. 8</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=35\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">glossary definition, printed p. 31</a>.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>A rubric describes what assessors examine in your evidence and explanation. Read it as a guide to the reasoning you need to make visible. This overview introduces the eight rubrics; the complete five-level descriptors appear with the relevant parts below.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Step, Part, Purpose, Rubric focus\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Identify an equity gap.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\"><strong>1.1</strong></a>: Analyze three years of numerical data and justify the selected group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\"><strong>1.2</strong></a>: Analyze three qualitative sources and connect findings to the numbers.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\"><strong>1.3</strong></a>: Explain the equity gap and its relationship to school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Examine factors and define the problem.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\"><strong>1.4</strong></a>: Support potential contributing factors with evidence and research.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\"><strong>1.5</strong></a>: Develop a focused, feasible problem responsive to the group's needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Propose and refine strategies.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\"><strong>1.6</strong></a>: Connect strategies to findings, factors, the problem, and school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\"><strong>1.7</strong></a>: Apply feedback, build partner support, and anticipate implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Examine leadership learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\"><strong>1.8</strong></a>: Explain evidence-supported leadership strengths and growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Investigate:</strong> Examine an attendance disparity alongside students' experiences and access to support.</li><li><strong>Plan:</strong> Examine whether unclear referral responsibilities interfere with timely assistance.</li><li><strong>Act:</strong> Seek feedback on a proposed process and adjust it for accessibility and staffing.</li><li><strong>Reflect:</strong> Explain how investigating access changed your understanding and identified a learning need.</li></ul>\n<p>Several responses and tables may contribute to the same rubric. The rubric evaluates their combined evidence and reasoning; one phrase or example does not establish a score. In the complete rubrics, <strong>OR</strong> identifies alternative shortcomings, while <strong>All of Level 3, plus</strong> and <strong>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus</strong> mean that higher levels retain the earlier requirements.</p></section><section id=\"fictional-case\"><h2>Fictional Example Case <span class=\"c1-case-link\">(For practice with real public data, see <a href=\"#equity-gap-help\">Equity Gap Help</a>.)</span></h2>\n<p>The example below uses fictional Cedar Grove Elementary, a K-5 school. Its selected indicator is Chronic Absenteeism, and its selected group is students with disabilities. All school figures, district and state comparisons, interviews, observations, records, resource commitments, and feedback in this case are invented for instruction.</p>\n<p>The case develops the same concern across Parts A-D so you can examine how the evidence supports the writing. The figures and responses illustrate reasoning; your own analysis must come from your school's evidence. The separately retained Jordan High public-data example and Harbor High fictional teaching extension provide additional examples with their original identities and qualifications.</p>\n<p>For this simplified dataset, chronic absenteeism means absence for at least 10% of enrolled instructional days. The denominator is the eligible student population in the fictional extract. The example does not reproduce every state reporting rule. Its labeled years show how a three-year table is organized; verify the latest required years and definitions for your actual setting.</p></section><section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A - Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages of responses, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.1, 1.2, 1.3.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S1_A_DataCollectionEquityGapAnalysis.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part A brings together numerical data, qualitative evidence, and the school's vision, mission, or goals. Its purpose is to develop a supported explanation of an equity concern. You first organize the evidence in Sections I-III, then explain your decisions and analysis in Section IV.</p><p>The first template page shows the quantitative table and student-group selection, followed by the qualitative table. The second page begins with school commitments and continues to the five analysis questions. The pictures retain the original layout; the exact numbered prompts also appear in readable text beside the guidance below.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part A template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>Part A has four sections: I. Initial Data Collection; II. Extended Data Collection; III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals; IV. Equity Gap Analysis. Begin with the evidence tables, then explain the decisions and relationships in the written responses.</p>\n<section><h3>Read the numbers before choosing an explanation</h3><p>Quantitative data are numerical observations: counts, percentages, rates, or scores. Check what is being counted and who is included. The <strong>numerator</strong> is the count meeting the condition; the <strong>denominator</strong> is the relevant population used to calculate the rate. In the latest fictional year, 30 chronically absent students divided by 100 eligible students gives 30%. A count of enrolled students may differ from the denominator used in a particular report.</p><p>Read both change over time and differences within the same year. A schoolwide improvement may coexist with a persistent group disparity. The schoolwide rate includes the selected group; it is a descriptive reference, not an independent comparison group. Students can appear in more than one reporting category, so subgroup rows generally cannot be added together.</p><p>A blank or suppressed result is not zero. Retain the source's explanation, examine counts as well as rates, and do not reconstruct suppressed information. Keep the same metric and confirm historical definitions before comparing years. Three annual groups are not necessarily the same students followed over time.</p><p>The template lists common student groups and permits rows to be added or deleted as needed. Examine relevant available groups before selecting one. If you investigate a combined group, such as a particular grade within a language group, you need evidence for that actual combination; adding two separate subgroup rows cannot create it.</p></section><h3>I. Initial Data Collection</h3>\n<p>The original table puts the most recent year first. The template permits adding or deleting student-group rows; the table below is a filled instructional selection, not a complete school extract. Each cell gives the rate and count / eligible students. Additional groups in a real school should be examined as relevant; missing or suppressed data must not be recorded as zero.</p>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student group</th><th scope=\"col\">2025-26: most recent fictional year</th><th scope=\"col\">2024-25</th><th scope=\"col\">2023-24</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">All students</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">18% (90/500)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">20% (100/500)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">22% (110/500)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Students with disabilities</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">30% (30/100)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">32% (32/100)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">34% (34/100)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">English learners</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">20% (16/80)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">22.5% (18/80)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">25% (20/80)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Socioeconomically disadvantaged</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">22% (66/300)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">24% (72/300)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">26% (78/300)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Students without disabilities</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">15% (60/400)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">17% (68/400)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">19% (76/400)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All-student counts include the selected group. English learner, disability, and socioeconomic categories overlap, so these rows cannot be summed. The without-disabilities row is the fictional complement of the disability row, not a separate official Dashboard field claimed here. Rates use the same fictional definition in each year, but the student membership can change.</p>\n<h3>Three views modeled on Equity Gap</h3>\n<p>The saved Equity Gap report includes available-year trends, same-year school/district/state comparisons, and subgroup gaps. These charts mirror those analytical views using fictional data. They are newly plotted illustrations, not screenshots or exports from the app. <a href=\"sources/010_full_report_jordan_high.txt\">Compare these chart types with the saved app report</a>.</p>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/attendance-trend.png\" alt=\"Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"896\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1. Both groups improve four percentage points, but the difference remains 12 points.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/system-comparison.png\" alt=\"Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"896\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1-T2. A favorable schoolwide comparison can conceal a persistent within-school gap. The fictional district and state are contextual references, not matched controls.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/subgroup-gaps.png\" alt=\"Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"928\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1. Positive values mean more chronic absenteeism than schoolwide. The chart identifies questions; it cannot identify causes.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Latest-year comparison</th><th scope=\"col\">Chronically absent / eligible</th><th scope=\"col\">Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">School (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">90 / 500</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">18%</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">District (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">1,000 / 5,000</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">20%</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">State (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">110,000 / 500,000</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">22%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><a href=\"sources/cycle1-fictional-attendance.csv\" download=\"\">Download the fictional counts, denominators, rates, and source labels</a>. The CSV is the numerical source for every chart. No real school data have been relabeled as fictional.</p>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><h3>I. Initial Data Collection - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Name one group after reviewing the numerical pattern.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, the three-year table and trend chart.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I selected students with disabilities. Their chronic absenteeism rate exceeds the schoolwide rate by 12 percentage points in all three fictional years. Both rates decline by four points, so overall improvement has not narrowed this difference. This is a reason to investigate access and support.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The choice follows the evidence and keeps one group in view. Disability status is a reporting category, not a causal explanation.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Choosing a group first and searching only for numbers that confirm an assumption.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1</a>.</p></section>\n<section><h3>Use each source to answer a specific question</h3><p>Qualitative evidence helps you understand experiences, processes, and context. It can include interview accounts, observations, and analysis of school documents. Decide what you need to understand before selecting the sources. Three quotations from the same interview do not create three different sources.</p><p>Ask about concrete experiences: what happened when a student or family tried to obtain support, what response they received, and what helped or hindered access. During observations, record what you see and hear before adding an interpretation. For document analysis, examine how a policy, schedule, procedure, or record describes responsibilities and actual follow-through.</p><p>Analyze the evidence by comparing recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. Explain how you selected participants or records, whose perspectives may be missing, and how access or language needs shaped participation. Counts of comments can describe your small sample, but they do not estimate how common an experience is across the entire school.</p><p>Follow your program and site procedures when collecting evidence. Keep identifiable records in authorized storage and protect identities in submitted materials. The school's required permissions and the candidates' own evidence cannot be supplied by a fictional example.</p></section><h3>II. Extended Data Collection</h3>\n<p>The original qualitative table has two columns: Data Sources and Qualitative Data. Record observations or accounts and then analyze their meaning. Interview themes, observed processes, and document content supply different evidence.</p>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Data Sources</th><th scope=\"col\">Qualitative Data</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q1. Eight volunteer student-caregiver interviews. Fictional notes; prompts ask about a recent attempt to obtain attendance support.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">Six accounts describe unclear contacts or arrangements they could not use; two describe helpful direct coordination. Example invented excerpts: \"I was told to call the other office, but did not know whom to ask for\" and \"Our contact called when I could answer and arranged the next step.\" These are contrasting experiences, not prevalence estimates.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q2. Three morning arrival observations. Fictional field notes from the school office.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">On two mornings, an arriving family was redirected between offices before the responsible adult was identified. On the third, a named staff member made a direct handoff. The notes document process variation; they do not explain annual absence or establish disability status by appearance. The teaching case stipulates that these encounters concern the selected group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q3. Document analysis of G1, the referral procedure, and six selected referral records for the group. Fictional records.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">The plan allocates follow-up time and names response deadlines. The procedure has a referral step but no explicit owner for confirming completion. Four records lack documented completion; two name an adult and record a completed arrangement. Missing documentation leaves delivery uncertain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A real inquiry would plan accessible participation and collect viewpoints beyond volunteers where appropriate. The examples above are a small teaching packet. More interviews or observations could challenge the interpretation.</p>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part A template, page 2. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure><section><h3>III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals</h3><p>Locate the actual school commitment relevant to your indicator and student group. A plan, mission statement, or improvement goal can establish the intended outcome and resources, but its presence does not demonstrate that the promised service occurred. Record the document and relevant language, then explain the connection to your inquiry.</p><p>The fictional excerpt below is specific enough to examine: it identifies a priority group, an allocation of staff time, and response commitments. Your real school's wording may be different. Use its verified commitments rather than inventing a goal that happens to match your preferred strategy.</p></section><h3>G1 - Fictional school commitment and allocation</h3>\n<blockquote><p>The school improvement plan identifies students with disabilities as a priority group for reducing chronic absenteeism. It allocates two weekly staff periods to attendance follow-up. A referred family should have a named contact within two school days and an agreed accessible support arrangement within five school days.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The two-day and five-day targets are invented school commitments, not CalAPA rules. Compare G1 with Q3: an allocation and a promise do not establish that families received usable support.</p>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><h3>III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Document a specific school commitment and its associated resources.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> G1, the fictional improvement-plan excerpt below.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The fictional school plan commits to reducing chronic absenteeism for students with disabilities and providing accessible attendance support. It assigns two weekly staff periods for family follow-up, a named contact within two school days of referral, and an agreed support arrangement within five school days. The concern is whether these allocated supports reach the intended students.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The stated goal becomes something the analysis can examine: allocation, access, monitoring, and outcome.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Inventing a real school goal or claiming that a plan proves services were delivered.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section>\n<p class=\"note\">The original Part A IV.1 prompt refers to quantitative data in Section II, although the quantitative table is in Section I. The original wording is retained below and in the images.</p>\n<section><h3>IV. Explain the decisions behind the evidence</h3><p>The five questions in Section IV ask you to explain how you moved from collected information to an equity-gap analysis. Refer to the tables, interpret the relationships, and make clear why the evidence matters. You can cite a finding again when it serves a different purpose, but avoid repeating the whole table in every response.</p><p>Analysis goes beyond description. \"The group rate is 30%\" describes a value. Explaining that both rates improved while their difference remained 12 percentage points identifies a relationship. Connecting that relationship with evidence about access and school commitments develops the equity analysis.</p><p>A percentage-point difference is found by subtraction: 30% minus 18% equals 12 percentage points. A percent change answers a different question and uses a denominator. Name the comparison explicitly so the reader does not confuse improvement over time with the difference between groups.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain the three-year pattern, why you collected these data, and why the group warrants investigation.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1-T2 and all three charts.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Schoolwide chronic absenteeism declined from 22% (110/500) to 20% (100/500) to 18% (90/500). For students with disabilities it declined from 34% (34/100) to 32% (32/100) to 30% (30/100). Each declined four percentage points; the gap remained 12 points. I used the same indicator across three years to examine persistence. The latest schoolwide rate is below the fictional district and state rates, but that favorable comparison does not resolve the within-school disparity. The rates describe annual groups, not the same children followed over time.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It interprets improvement and persistent disparity together. Numerators, denominators, years, and comparison choices remain visible.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Writing \"12 percent higher\" when the intended comparison is 12 percentage points. The relative difference in the latest year is a different calculation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1</a>.</p></section><section id=\"qualitative-analysis\"><h3>From a record to a bounded finding</h3><p>This is one possible way to analyze qualitative evidence, not a required coding procedure, sample size, software package, or additional upload. All excerpts and observations are invented within the Cedar Grove case.</p><p><strong>Record:</strong> one of the Q1 accounts says, \"I was told to call the other office, but did not know whom to ask for.\" Preserve the account as a report of that person's experience. A tentative category is <em>unclear handoff responsibility</em>; avoid the unsupported label \"family disengagement.\"</p><p><strong>Compare:</strong> another Q1 account describes a contact calling at a usable time and arranging the next step. Q2 likewise includes a direct handoff on one of three observed mornings. These contrasting cases challenge a claim that support was always inaccessible.</p><p><strong>Check another source:</strong> Q3 finds four of six selected records without documented completion and two with it. Missing documentation leaves delivery uncertain; it does not prove no help occurred.</p><p><strong>Bounded finding:</strong> coordination appears inconsistent in this small selected packet. The evidence warrants examining how the promised support reaches students with disabilities. It cannot estimate schoolwide prevalence or explain how much of the 12-point attendance disparity the process caused. Read this alongside G1's resource and response commitments in the equity-gap synthesis.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Describe three different qualitative sources and what each contributes.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1 interviews, Q2 observations, Q3 document analysis.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I compared eight volunteer student-caregiver interviews, three morning arrival observations, and a review of the attendance plan, referral procedure, and six referral records. Interviews showed how support was experienced; observations showed how arrival handoffs operated; documents showed intended responsibilities and recorded follow-through. These sources were selected to examine how attendance supports reached students with disabilities. They are small purposeful samples, not representative estimates.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The sources have different jobs and address the same selected group and indicator.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Counting three quotations from one interview as three different sources.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Compare patterns across sources and explain their relationship to the numbers.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3, including the successful support cases.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Six interview accounts described difficulty identifying a contact or arranging a usable support time. Two described successful direct help. On two observed mornings, staff redirected a family between offices before identifying the responsible adult; the third morning included a direct handoff. Four reviewed referral records lacked documented follow-through; two included it. These findings suggest inconsistent access and monitoring alongside a persistent attendance disparity. Missing documentation does not prove that no help occurred, and an arrival delay is not itself a chronically absent classification.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Convergence and counterexamples narrow the claim. Qualitative events are not incorrectly treated as the annual outcome measure.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Turning six volunteer accounts into \"75% of all families\" or saying the observations prove why students were absent.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2</a>, <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Define the equity gap by connecting outcomes, school resources, access, and relevant research.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, and the research connection.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The equity concern is a persistent 12-point chronic-absence disparity for students with disabilities alongside inconsistent access to the follow-up support promised in the school plan. Staff time and response commitments are allocated, but the reviewed experiences and records do not show reliable access or monitoring. The attendance-messaging study by <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a> supports examining communication as one potentially modifiable process. It does not establish the cause of this disparity or the effect of this proposed referral procedure. Health needs, transportation, and attendance-record accuracy remain questions for additional inquiry.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The definition includes the mismatch between intended support and experienced access. The research has a stated use and a stated limit.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Defining equity only as a subtraction, or calling the proposed access mechanism a proven cause.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p><p><a href=\"#cycle/c1/b-q3\">Continue to the revised research connection in Part B, question 3</a>, which tests and narrows this initial interpretation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain the exact connection to the school commitment documented in Section III.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, G1, Q1, and Q3.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The fictional plan explicitly names students with disabilities in its attendance goal and provides staff time and response deadlines for accessible support. The 30% latest-year rate and 12-point disparity show the outcome concern. Incomplete follow-through records and mixed family experiences raise a question about whether the allocation is fulfilling that commitment. I would examine actual delivery and accessibility before concluding that the school simply needs more services.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It connects the school promise to evidence of delivery and outcomes.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Saying only \"this aligns with our mission of success for all.\"</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section>\n<h3>Research connection and its boundary</h3><p>The IES randomized study of approximately 26,000 K-5 students found that adaptive parent messaging reduced chronic absence without improving achievement in the study year (<a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a>). This supports investigating communication and response processes. It does not identify the cause of Cedar Grove's disparity or establish effects specifically for students with disabilities.</p>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-1\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><p>Essential Question: Based on the chosen California state indicator, how well does the candidate select and analyze quantitative data sources across the three most recent years, identify patterns and/or trends related to equity, and choose an appropriate student group?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not include quantitative data across the three most recent years. OR Patterns and trends are not identified or are irrelevant. OR Candidate does not select a student group to investigate OR selects a student group not appropriate for the identified indicator.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Essential evidence is absent or irrelevant; the OR conditions are alternatives.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects a California state indicator* and minimally analyzes quantitative data, vaguely identifying patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate is not clear about which student group they will investigate or why the student group was selected.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Some numerical analysis is present, but the pattern or choice of group remains unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects a California state indicator* and analyzes quantitative data across the three most recent years, clearly identifying general patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate clearly identifies a student group to investigate further and provides a rationale for why this student group was selected.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Analyze three years, identify an equity pattern, and justify one group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explores additional data linked to the indicator that clearly informs their understanding of patterns and/or trends to further clarify group differences within the state indicator selected (e.g., demographic data, ethnicity, gender, language).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Add relevant data that actually clarify within-indicator group differences.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant research that informs their understanding (patterns and/or trends related to equity) of the determined equity issue for the chosen student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use relevant research to inform the deeper quantitative interpretation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Note: *California state indicator: Chronic Absenteeism, Suspension Rate, English Learner Progress, Graduation Rate, Academic Performance, College/Career Readiness Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 10. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The three-year table and A IV.1 explain the persistent disparity and group selection. The system comparison adds context, but another chart alone does not demonstrate a higher level. Relevant research must inform the interpretation, not simply appear in a citation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-2\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate collect and analyze relevant qualitative data from a range of at least three different qualitative* data sources and explain their relationship of the findings from the analysis to the quantitative** data findings and the identified student group equity issue?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide or analyze three qualitative* data sources. OR Candidate provides no or irrelevant information about the connection among qualitative and quantitative data** findings and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Three sources are missing or unanalyzed, or the connection to the equity issue is missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate collects and minimally analyzes a range of qualitative* data from at least three sources. Candidate minimally connects the qualitative data and findings to the quantitative data** findings for the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Sources and connections receive only minimal analysis.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate collects and analyzes a range of relevant qualitative* data from three different sources. Candidate clearly explains the relationship among the findings from the analyses of the qualitative data, the quantitative data,** and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Analyze three different relevant sources and explain how findings relate to the numbers and group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate collects additional qualitative data as appropriate to deepen their understanding of the California state indicator chosen and the student group equity issue, and provides a complete analysis of the relationship between their qualitative and quantitative data findings and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Gather additional appropriate qualitative evidence and analyze how it deepens the connection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how their qualitative data collection strategy is responsive to the complex context in which they are working and demonstrates cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for diverse viewpoints.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how collection responds to context, cultural sensitivity, and diverse viewpoints.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Notes: *Qualitative: Collected through methods like interviews, focus groups, and observations, this data is subjective, rich in context, and commonly analyzed by identifying themes, patterns, or through coding (e.g., responses from interviews with students, faculty, or families/guardians; notes from observations; document analysis) **Quantitative: Numerical data expressing a certain quantity, amount, or range. Usually, there are measurement units associated with the data (e.g., meters, degrees, score points). Arithmetic and statistical operations may be applied to quantitative data. Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 11. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: Q1-Q3 provide three kinds of evidence and preserve successful as well as difficult experiences. The example does not claim to demonstrate the additional collection required at Level 4 or a complete culturally responsive collection strategy at Level 5.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-3\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate conduct an equity gap analysis based on the chosen California state indicator to inform their understanding of the equity issues for the identified student group? How does this equity gap align with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue with no evidence of quantitative or qualitative data analysis. OR Candidate does not identify any patterns and/or trends in the equity gap analysis provided. OR Candidate does not provide a description of an equity gap for the student group. OR Candidate does not identify the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The equity issue lacks necessary evidence, pattern, description, or school-goal alignment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group based on minimal quantitative or qualitative data analysis. Candidate identifies patterns and/or trends that are not clear in the equity gap analysis provided or does not clearly describe the identified patterns and/or trends. Candidate provides a minimal description of an equity gap for the student group. Candidate minimally describes the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The analysis, pattern, definition, or alignment is only minimally developed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group clearly based on their quantitative and qualitative data analysis for the chosen state indicator. Candidate clearly describes patterns and/or trends across the quantitative and qualitative data analysis in the equity gap analysis provided. Candidate clearly identifies the alignment between the equity gap for the identified student group and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Integrate quantitative and qualitative findings and align the issue to a specific school commitment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate conducts a thorough equity gap analysis, describing a clear connection from quantitative data findings to supportive qualitative data findings, and provides a sophisticated understanding of the equity disparity identified for the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain the relationships thoroughly to show a deeper understanding of the disparity.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites and explains how research informs their understanding of the equity gap for the specific student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how research informs understanding of this group's equity gap.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Elements 3B, 3C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 12. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: G1 connects resources and school commitments to the combined findings. The research connection has a stated limitation for the selected group; it does not establish the cause of this particular disparity.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Can you justify your selected group using the three-year pattern?</li><li>Have you analyzed three different qualitative sources and connected them to the numerical evidence?</li><li>Does the equity-gap explanation address resources, access, school commitments, and outcomes, including uncertainty?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-investigate/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: investigate</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B - Contributing Factors and Problem Statement</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.4 and 1.5. Use evidence from Step 1 in each response. Embed research citations in the responses; the official instructions say not to provide a separate citation list.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S2_B_ContribFactorsAndProbStatement.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part B develops the explanation begun in Part A. A <strong>contributing factor</strong> is a condition that may help explain the identified equity gap. It must be supported by evidence and relevant research. Student identity is not, by itself, an explanation.</p><p><strong>Institutional factors</strong> can include established norms or practices that shape opportunities. <strong>Structural factors</strong> concern how responsibilities, schedules, decisions, and resources are organized. A referral procedure with no clear follow-up owner is a possible structural condition. A longstanding assumption that every family can attend a daytime appointment may be an institutional practice worth examining. These categories can overlap; explain how the actual condition operates rather than concentrating on its label.</p><p>Begin by comparing plausible explanations and checking them against Part A, including evidence that challenges your first interpretation. If the evidence does not clarify contributing factors, the manual directs you to collect more data or review relevant research. Then identify a need the school could address and develop one feasible problem statement. An <strong>educational need</strong> describes what students need to access or achieve; a <strong>strategy</strong> describes a proposed action to meet it.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part B template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Identify potential school-controlled conditions supported by Part A.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3 and the completed Part A analysis.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Two potential factors are unclear ownership of attendance-support referrals and support arrangements that families cannot readily use. Q1 accounts and Q2 office handoffs point to unclear contacts; Q3 shows missing follow-through documentation. The successful cases suggest that direct adult coordination is already possible. Health and transportation may also matter, but these sources do not establish their contribution to the annual disparity.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The proposed factors are institutional processes supported by multiple sources.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Treating disability, family values, or a preferred intervention as the contributing factor.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q2\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain a plausible mechanism connecting each condition to learning or well-being.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1 experience excerpts and Q2 observation notes.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>When responsibility is unclear, families may need to repeat requests before reaching an adult who can arrange help. A meeting time or contact format that a family cannot use may leave an identified barrier unresolved. These processes could prolong difficulty attending and reduce access to instruction. The observed office delays demonstrate friction in the support process, but they do not measure how many days of absence that friction produced.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It explains a mechanism and keeps the size of the effect uncertain.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Claiming that four incomplete records caused the 12-point gap.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q3\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Use research to examine the proposed mechanism and its limits.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The IES research connection introduced in Part A.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Initial draft: an incomplete research connection</p><p>The study by <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a> gives a reason to examine timely, usable family communication as one potentially modifiable condition. Its evidence does not establish the effectiveness of a referral-handoff procedure for students with disabilities. I therefore treat accessible communication and documented follow-through as proposals requiring local feasibility review, while seeking more directly matched research on disability-related attendance barriers.</p></blockquote><p>What still needs revision: the draft acknowledges a limitation but has not yet used research to examine the selected group's access needs. The next passage demonstrates that revision.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Adding an unrelated citation or implying that a general K-5 finding establishes a disability-specific effect.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p><h4>Revised research connection</h4><blockquote><p><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10803-023-06025-3\">Totsika and colleagues (2024; first published online in 2023)</a> studied parent-reported attendance among UK children aged 5-15 with intellectual disability and/or autism. Unmet school needs and the parent-teacher relationship were relevant to attendance. This broadens my question from whether families receive reminders to whether the offered support fits the child and can be used. The survey is observational, pandemic-period, and narrower than our full disability reporting group; it does not establish Cedar Grove's causes or validate a referral procedure.</p><p>I therefore interpret Q1-Q3 as evidence of inconsistent coordination, not proof that disability or family motivation explains absence. The successful direct-contact cases suggest a process worth examining. The IES messaging study supports attention to communication; it does not establish that messaging alone addresses access. My proposed handoff would confirm a usable arrangement and coordinate existing supports. Health, transport, individual needs, and the effect on attendance remain to be investigated.</p></blockquote><p><strong>What changed:</strong> the first draft relied on a general communication study. The revision uses a more relevant population to reconsider the mechanism, then limits the claim to what the local records support. CalAPA does not require every study to sample only the selected group; explain relevance and transfer limits.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q4\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> State the educational need before selecting a program.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3 and G1.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Students with disabilities and their families need a dependable, accessible route from an attendance concern to a named adult and a usable support arrangement. Staff need clear handoff responsibilities and a way to verify that the intended support was received. This need can be addressed within the school attendance process while individual health, service, and transportation needs are examined with the appropriate staff.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The need is connected to resources and access, with a feasible school-level scope.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Listing new programs without showing what the students need from them.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>, <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q5\"><h3>II. Problem Statement - Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group\u2019s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Write one focused problem statement that follows from the investigation.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Part A, the potential factors, and the selected educational need.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>At fictional Cedar Grove Elementary, students with disabilities experience a persistent chronic-absence disparity: 30% compared with 18% schoolwide in the latest fictional year. Despite allocated follow-up time, reviewed family experiences, office handoffs, and referral records show inconsistent access to a named adult and confirmed support. The school needs a reliable process for arranging and checking accessible attendance support for this group. The initial improvement scope is the referral and follow-through process; the evidence does not explain every absence.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The statement names the group, outcome concern, access need, and feasible scope. Existing staff time and successful handoffs make this a plausible process to examine.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Writing \"buy an attendance app\" as the problem or promising to eliminate all absences.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-4\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to the identified equity gap affecting a student group and cite the research supporting their determination?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies contributing factors that are biased, superficial, or irrelevant to the equity gap analysis. OR Candidate does not cite research and/or neglects to draw connections between research and contributing factors.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Factors are biased, superficial, or irrelevant, or research is absent or disconnected.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies potential contributing factors and minimally describes how they relate to the equity gap analysis. Candidate attempts to draw connections between research and contributing factors, but citations are not related to the equity gap.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Connections between factors, findings, and research remain weak.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly uses the equity gap analysis and findings from the quantitative and qualitative data analyses to determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to an equity gap affecting a student group. Candidate cites relevant research to support potential contributing factors that create or add to the identified equity gap affecting a student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use both kinds of evidence and relevant research to support institutional or structural factors.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains in detail (with supporting evidence from Step 1) how several contributing factors can create or add to equity differences or disparities for a student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain in detail, with Step 1 evidence, how several factors may contribute.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate demonstrates a sophisticated, research-based understanding of the systemic, institutional, or structural causes of the identified single equity gap for a group of students at the school.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Demonstrate a sophisticated research-based understanding of systemic or structural causes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 15. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The example identifies ownership and access conditions supported by Q1-Q3. The revised Part B research connection uses attendance and neurodevelopmental-condition studies to refine the inquiry while distinguishing local evidence, observational associations, and limits of transfer.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-5\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate use the equity gap analysis and identification of potential contributing factors to develop a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not use the equity gap analysis or potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement. OR Candidate\u2019s problem statement is not responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The problem does not follow from the investigation or does not respond to the group's needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate attempts to use the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement, but it is not clear how the problem statement supports student achievement and/or well-being of the student group. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is only partially responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Its connection to achievement or well-being, or its responsiveness, is unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate develops a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being of the student group that clearly draws from the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors identified. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is clearly responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Develop a feasible, responsive problem statement grounded in the analysis and factors.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains why their problem statement is responsive and feasible for the school\u2019s culture and context.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain feasibility and responsiveness in the school's culture and context.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant evidence-based practices or research on how the area of educational need has been addressed in other school settings to improve achievement and/or well-being for similar student groups.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use evidence-based practice or research from other settings with similar student groups.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 16. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The problem statement identifies a need the school can examine within existing staff time. Evidence from other settings with similar student groups would need to be evaluated before claiming the additional research connection described at Level 5.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does each potential factor have a clear connection to Step 1 evidence and relevant research?</li><li>Have you considered another explanation and contradictory evidence?</li><li>Does the problem statement identify a responsive, feasible school-level need?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-plan/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: plan</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C - Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.6 and 1.7. Develop strategies, obtain targeted leader feedback, explain adjustments, and plan for buy-in and implications.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S3_C_PlanSchoolImprovementPromotEquity.docx\" download>Download the original Part C template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part C turns the problem statement into proposed action. A <strong>strategy</strong> explains what adults would do, how it responds to the investigated need, and what would make it feasible in this school. You need a clear connection between the evidence, the contributing conditions, and each proposed action.</p><p>Cycle 1's Act step includes obtaining targeted feedback from an administrator, supervisor, or educational leader familiar with the school. Arrange that conversation after you have a proposal specific enough to discuss. Ask about each strategy's appropriateness, feasibility, needed partners, and resources. Record the substance of the feedback and explain how you would adjust or strengthen the proposal. A plan to ask for feedback later does not supply evidence that you received it.</p><p><strong>Educational partners</strong> are the people whose knowledge, participation, or decisions matter to the work, including staff, students, families, and relevant community partners. <strong>Buy-in</strong> develops when people understand the proposal and have a meaningful chance to question and shape it. Explain that process and anticipate practical effects such as workload, scheduling, accessibility, and coordination. The fictional records below illustrate these decisions; the actual feedback in your submission must come from your own work.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part C template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<h3>F1 - Fictional feedback and proposed adjustments</h3>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Initial proposal</th><th scope=\"col\">Fictional targeted feedback</th><th scope=\"col\">Proposed adjustment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Send attendance reminders and mark them sent.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Principal: delivery does not establish that a family has usable support.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Confirm a named contact, preferred communication, and an agreed arrangement; record alternate attempts if contact is not made.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Introduce the process schoolwide at once.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Principal: the two staff periods may not cover the workload.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Pilot in one grade band and review staff capacity weekly.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Schedule another attendance meeting for each referral.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Special education lead: another meeting may duplicate existing support and add an access burden.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Check existing arrangements and coordinate before requesting another meeting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Proposed evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Question it could answer</th><th scope=\"col\">What it would not establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Referral and follow-through log</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Was a responsible adult assigned, and was support arranged?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">That the arrangement caused better attendance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Family confirmation and preferred-format feedback</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Could the family use the arrangement?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">That all families experienced it the same way.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Workload review and unresolved-barrier notes</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Is the process feasible, and what remains unresolved?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">A schoolwide effect after a short pilot.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Later attendance records with denominators</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">How do absence patterns develop?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">Causation from a before/after comparison alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section id=\"strategy-map\"><h3>Proposed action and evidence map</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Proposed action, Local evidence, Intended access change, Feedback adjustment, Still untested\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Proposed action</th><th scope=\"col\">Local evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Intended access change</th><th scope=\"col\">Feedback adjustment</th><th scope=\"col\">Still untested</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Name a handoff owner</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">Q1 unclear contacts; Q2 redirections; Q3 incomplete records</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">A family reaches an adult who can arrange help</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: confirm receipt and a usable arrangement, not only a sent message</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Whether support is received consistently or attendance improves</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Agree on usable contact and support arrangements</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">Q1 contrasting experiences; G1 accessible-support commitment</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">Contact timing and format fit the family and student</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: coordinate existing supports before adding meetings</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Which arrangements fit individual needs</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Review follow-through during allocated staff time</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">G1 allocation; four incomplete Q3 records</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">Unresolved referrals receive attention</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: start with one grade band and review workload</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Capacity, equitable reach, and any outcome effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This map organizes the fictional proposal and feedback for Part C questions 1-4. It is a teaching aid, not another required submission.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q1\"><h3>Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Describe what adults would do, when, and with what resources.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The Part B problem and G1 staffing allocation.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I propose a named attendance contact for each referral, confirmation of the family's preferred language and accessible communication method, an agreed support appointment, and a documented check that the arrangement was usable. A designated staff member would review unresolved referrals during the two existing weekly follow-up periods. Appropriate personnel would examine transport, health, or service concerns when identified. A six-week pilot would examine feasibility and access.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The proposal identifies a process and resources. Its timeline is a proposed local decision, not a CalAPA requirement.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Announcing \"improve communication\" without assigning responsibility or checking access.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q2\"><h3>Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain how each proposed action responds to the problem.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, F1, and the <a href=\"#cycle/c1/strategy-map\">proposed action and evidence map</a>.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Naming an adult responds to repeated uncertainty about who could help. Agreeing on a usable contact method and appointment responds to access problems in the interviews. Recording the follow-through responds to the monitoring gap in four reviewed records. These changes aim to make the existing allocation reach the selected group. They do not guarantee a reduction in the annual chronic-absence rate.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Each action has a specific evidence-based rationale.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Choosing actions from a generic improvement menu without returning to Part A.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q3\"><h3>Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors\u2014including institutional and/or structural factors\u2014and how they align with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Connect strategies to contributing factors and school commitments.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> G1, B1-B4, and the proposed process.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The proposal addresses unclear ownership and inconsistent support access by assigning the handoff and confirming whether it worked. It uses the two staff periods already allocated in G1 and checks the plan's two-day contact and five-day arrangement commitments. Family preferences and disability-related access needs would guide arrangements. Staff would examine whether workload allows these commitments before expanding the process.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The strategy addresses organizational conditions and the documented goal.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Assuming the existing schedule has spare capacity without checking it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q4\"><h3>Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Report actual targeted feedback in a real submission and explain proposed adjustments.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1, the explicitly fictional feedback record.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>In the fictional feedback conversation, the principal warned that a message-sent checkbox could be mistaken for accessible support. I would add family confirmation or a documented alternate contact attempt. The principal also asked whether two staff periods could cover the workload, so I would start with one grade band and review capacity weekly. The special education lead advised coordinating with existing support arrangements; I would add that check before proposing another meeting.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The example shows feedback on the proposed strategies and consequential adjustments. These conversations are invented teaching evidence.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Presenting a future request for feedback as completed feedback, or writing only \"the principal approved.\"</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q5\"><h3>Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Plan meaningful partner involvement and anticipate practical consequences.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1 and the revised strategy.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Before a pilot, I would ask families and students to review contact wording and timing, ask office staff and teachers to test the handoff, and ask the special education lead to check access and coordination. I would record disagreements and revisions before agreeing on responsibilities. Anticipated issues include staff workload, inaccessible communication, duplicate contacts, and interpreting unresolved health barriers as noncompliance. A weekly review would examine access and workload before expanding the process.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> People can influence the proposal; the plan anticipates feasible implementation concerns.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Treating an announcement or signature as evidence of buy-in.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-6\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><p>Essential Question: How are the strategy(ies) proposed for equitable school improvement for the student group well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis, including contributing factors, and responsive to the problem statement? How is the proposed strategy(ies) aligned to the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not propose strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement informed by the findings. OR Proposed strategy(ies) are not aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Strategies are not informed by the findings or aligned to the school's commitments.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) are minimally informed by the findings and provide only general reference to the equity gap analysis, contributing factors, and/or problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are partially aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The connection to findings and goals is general or partial.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement for the student group are well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis and the contributing factors are responsive to the problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are clearly aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Connect strategies clearly to the gap analysis, factors, problem, and school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides relevant strategy(ies) that strategically focus on equitable student and school improvement and represent a contextually responsive approach to addressing the equity issue or educational need.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Show how the approach responds strategically to this particular context.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides research-based evidence of the relevance of the proposed strategy(ies) and their implementation for improving student achievement and/or well-being for the specific student group and school.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Provide research-based evidence for the strategy and implementation relevant to this group and school.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 18. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=22\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The proposed handoff responds to the evidence and G1 allocation. Its effect has not been evaluated in this fictional case; research about messaging does not validate the entire adapted procedure.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-7\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate apply the feedback received from administrators or educational leaders familiar with the school culture and context and describe next steps for creating educational partner buy-in and potential implications for the adjusted set of strategy(ies)?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not apply feedback from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen their proposed strategy(ies). OR Candidate states their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with little or no explanation of steps for buy-in from educational partner(s). OR Candidate does not identify anticipated implications for proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Feedback is not used, steps for buy-in are poorly explained, or implications are missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely describes feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader and makes irrelevant and/or inappropriate adjustments to proposed strategy(ies). Candidate vaguely describes their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with educational partner(s); it is not clear that educational partner(s) will have the opportunity to develop buy-in. Candidate does not clearly describe anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Feedback, adjustments, partner involvement, or likely consequences remain vague.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly applies feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen proposed strategy(ies). Candidate clearly provides relevant and appropriate next steps for creating buy-in and communicating with educational partner(s) to address the equity gap and learning need(s). Candidate clearly describes anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to the implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Apply relevant feedback, explain appropriate buy-in steps, and anticipate realistic implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate seeks additional rounds of feedback from other administrators or educational partners on the revised strategy(ies) and develops a detailed strategic plan to communicate and share the plan with a diverse range of educational partners to ensure they are proposing a workable/feasible approach to addressing the equity gap and learning need.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Seek further feedback on revisions and develop a detailed plan involving diverse partners.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate plans to coach educational partners to examine and address potential biases that could impact student learning and/or well-being due to identified equity gaps, including those specifically related to sources of education disadvantage or discrimination, and is transparent about the potential underlying contributing factors.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Plan to coach partners to examine biases and disadvantage while being transparent about factors.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1B, 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C CAPE Standard 6; Elements 6A, 6B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 19. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=23\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: F1 makes the feedback and proposed adjustment visible. Proposed future partner conversations are not completed additional rounds of feedback. A Level 4 claim would require the further feedback described in that descriptor.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Have you explained the application and rationale for each proposed strategy?</li><li>Can you identify actual targeted leader feedback and the proposed adjustment it prompted?</li><li>Have you planned how partners can influence the proposal and anticipated realistic implications?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-act/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: act</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section><section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D - Reflective Narrative</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 written pages OR up to 5 minutes of video explanation. <b>Rubric:</b> 1.8. The video option is your reflective explanation; Cycle 1 does not require the meeting clips used in later cycles.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S4_D_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part D template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part D asks you to examine what the cycle taught you about your leadership. Refer to specific decisions and evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Explain your initial understanding, what challenged or strengthened it, and how it influenced your choices.</p><p>A useful reflection includes both a strength and an area for growth. Naming a limitation does not cancel your accomplishment; it helps explain what you have learned and what you need to practice next. Consider how the school's social, economic, or cultural context affected your approach and whose perspectives changed a decision.</p><p>Use the same three prompts whether you write or choose the video explanation. For video, plan your evidence references and check that all three prompts fit within five minutes; speak naturally about your own work. For either format, distinguish what you learned from what your proposed strategy might accomplish. A revised proposal is evidence of your reasoning, not evidence that the student outcome has already improved.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part D template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q1\"><h3>Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain why you sought that leader's feedback and how it changed your approach to buy-in.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1 and the before-and-after decisions in Part C.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I sought the principal's feedback because she controls staff time and understands office responsibilities. The special education lead could test whether the process coordinated with existing supports and access needs. Their fictional feedback changed my initial emphasis on sending reminders to confirming usable support. It also changed buy-in from announcing a finished procedure to asking families and staff to test it before adoption.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The rationale explains the feedback providers' relevance and a specific change in participation.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Naming a leader without explaining why their knowledge mattered.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q2\"><h3>Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain a leadership strength and growth need with evidence from the cycle.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, and F1.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>A strength was recognizing that declining schoolwide absence did not remove the persistent subgroup disparity. Comparing the successful and unsuccessful support cases also kept me from treating every family experience as identical. A growth need was my initial assumption that clearer reminders were enough. The interviews and feedback pushed me to consider time, language, accessibility, and whether a response reached the intended student. I strengthened a proposal; I have no implementation results demonstrating improved attendance.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Reflection connects decisions, evidence, context, and limits.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Reporting that the gap closed when no strategy has been implemented.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q3\"><h3>Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Name a professional learning goal, actions, and evidence of your growth.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The limitation identified in D2.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Over the next six weeks, I will practice checking the difference between offering support and establishing that it is accessible. In two planning meetings I will record the evidence considered, whose perspective is missing, an alternative explanation, and any decision changed by family or student input. A mentor will review both decision logs with me. I will evaluate whether those perspectives changed my reasoning and the proposal, while continuing to examine disability-specific attendance research.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The goal is observable and grows directly from the reflection.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Promising to \"be a better communicator\" without a practice or a way to examine it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-8\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><p>Essential Question: How did the leadership feedback shape the candidate\u2019s equity-focused strategy, build educational partner buy-in, and support their growth in addressing equity needs and gaps for the identified student group? How does the candidate reflect on their current strengths and identify future areas for growth in equity leadership?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not discuss or only mentions their selected administrator/supervisor/educational leader and/or the importance of educational partner buy-in is not discussed in the reflection. OR Candidate does not reflect on their growth as an equity-driven leader and/or does not address the equity needs or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site. OR Candidate does not refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. OR Candidate does not discuss strengths or areas for future professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Key reflection elements or evidence are absent; any listed OR condition matters.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides limited insight into why they selected the administrator/supervisor/educational leader for feedback and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in. Candidate vaguely reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and minimally discusses their ability to address the equity needs and/or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate does not clearly explain their current strengths and/or vaguely mentions future areas for growth in equity leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The feedback rationale, learning, or strengths and growth are vague.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains their rationale for selecting the administrator/supervisor/educational leader to provide feedback on the selected strategy(ies) and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in from school personnel. Candidate clearly reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and discusses their ability to address the equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to specific, relevant evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate clearly explains their current strengths and identifies future areas for growth in equity leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain feedback selection, effects on buy-in, and evidence-supported strengths and growth.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s reflection demonstrates how the school context\u2014 including social, economic, or cultural contexts\u2014 impacts their approach to providing equity-driven leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how social, economic, or cultural context shapes equity leadership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate utilizes experiences from Steps 1, 2, and 3 in confronting equity issues and gaps to describe what leadership skills have been learned and how those skills may be used in the future.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use experiences from Steps 1-3 to explain learned leadership skills and future uses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part D: Reflective Narrative (up to 5 pages of written or up to 5 minutes of video explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B CAPE Standard 6; Element 6A</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 21. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The reflection uses the numerical pattern, qualitative counterexamples, and leader feedback to explain a strength and growth need. Context and future use of learned skills need explanation, not just labels.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the reflection refer to specific evidence and decisions from the cycle?</li><li>Have you explained why you selected the feedback provider and how feedback affected buy-in?</li><li>Does the learning goal name a practice, timeframe, and way to examine your growth?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-reflect/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: reflect</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section></section><section id=\"final-check\"><h2>Review the complete cycle</h2><p>Read Parts A-D together. Check that the indicator, student group, evidence, problem statement, strategies, and reflection remain consistent. Where your understanding changed, explain the evidence behind the change. Verify every prompt and subprompt, then check each part's length in the actual submission file.</p><p>Use pseudonyms and redact identifying information as the manual directs. Your submitted evidence cannot replace required uploads with hyperlinks. Embed research citations in the responses where the source informs your reasoning; Part A and B directions say not to provide a separate citation list. Review the official translation provisions when applicable, retain your files, and inspect the uploaded copies before submitting.</p><p><a href=\"#readiness\">Review submission preparation</a> &middot; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Complete official Cycle 1 manual</a></p></section>\n<section id=\"additional-examples\"><h2>Additional examples and source evidence</h2><p>The <a href=\"#connections/c1\">existing extended Cycle 1 example</a> combines public Jordan High graduation evidence with a separately labeled fictional Harbor High inquiry. Use it to compare reasoning across settings, not to combine figures or events from different schools. <a href=\"#charts/graduation-gaps\">Public graduation charts and source tables</a> retain their original school identity and measure qualifications.</p><p>The template images are rendered from the supplied CTC Version 09 Word files. All 20 numbered prompts and all 40 rubric descriptors are retained in readable text; line wrapping is normalized. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Official originals govern requirements.</p><p><a href=\"sources/cycle1-fictional-attendance.csv\" download>Fictional attendance data and denominators</a> &middot; <a href=\"#equity-gap-help\">Equity Gap Help</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c2\">Cycle 2</a></p></section></article>", "search": [{"title": "Cycle 1: Six indicators and equity-gap analysis", "url": "#cycle/c1/indicators", "html": "<section id=\"indicators\"><h2>Choose one of six California state indicators</h2><p>An <strong>indicator</strong> is an area of student learning or well-being that you investigate using data. CalAPA Cycle 1 requires you to select one of the following six indicators. You do not investigate all six in one submission.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Indicator, What you examine\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Indicator</th><th scope=\"col\">What you examine</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Chronic Absenteeism</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns of students missing substantial amounts of school and the resulting concern about access to learning.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Suspension Rate</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns of suspension, including differences among student groups.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">English Learner Progress</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">English learners' progress toward English language proficiency.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Graduation Rate</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Patterns in students completing high school, including differences among groups.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">Academic Performance</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Academic performance, including English language arts and mathematics.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Indicator\">College/Career Readiness</td><td data-label=\"What you examine\">Students' preparation for college and careers.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Begin with an indicator relevant to your school's grade levels and available evidence. Examine related quantitative data across the <strong>three most recent available years</strong>, then use that analysis to select <strong>one student group</strong> for closer investigation. If you cannot obtain three years for an indicator, the manual directs you to choose another indicator for which those data are available. Do not substitute an older convenient period without checking the requirement.</p>\n<p>An indicator and a student group are different things. Chronic Absenteeism is an indicator; students with disabilities is a possible student group. A disparity in that group's results gives you a reason to investigate. It does not tell you which school practice caused the difference.</p>\n<p>Start with official school and district records and sources such as the California School Dashboard, the Local Control and Accountability Plan (LCAP), Ed-Data, or the School Accountability Report Card (SARC). The <a href=\"#equity-gap-help\">Equity Gap Help</a> page shows how the separate app can assist with public-data exploration. Confirm the exact school, measure, reporting years, group definitions, counts, and denominators before using a figure.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=10\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Official indicator and three-year requirements, printed pp. 6-7</a>.</p></section>\n<section id=\"equity-gap\"><h2>What an equity gap means</h2>\n<p>In plain language, an equity gap concerns differences in students' access to educational opportunities, resources, support, or outcomes that warrant examining whether the school is meeting their needs fairly. CalAPA asks you to examine a specific relationship: the resources and intended outcomes identified in school or district improvement plans, and actual performance for underserved students.</p>\n<p>This means examining <strong>school commitments, resource allocations, access to support, and outcomes together</strong>. A school may fund an attendance-support program and still need to investigate whether the intended students can use it and whether anyone checks its effectiveness. An equity-gap analysis may reveal incomplete evidence or weak monitoring as well as a difference in outcomes.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Evidence, What it helps establish\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">What it helps establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">A fictional group has 30% chronic absenteeism, compared with 18% schoolwide.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">A 12-percentage-point outcome difference warrants investigation.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">The school plan promises accessible support and allocates staff time.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">There is a stated commitment and an allocation to examine.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Evidence\">Interviews, observations, and records suggest inconsistent follow-through.</td><td data-label=\"What it helps establish\">Access and monitoring may need attention; the cause of the outcome difference is not established.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Your analysis explains what the combined evidence supports and what remains uncertain. The numerical disparity alone does not establish an inequitable practice or its cause. Likewise, the existence of a program does not demonstrate that students received usable support. This is why Part A includes numerical evidence, qualitative evidence, and the school's goals.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\"><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=12\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Equity-gap analysis, printed p. 8</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=35\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">glossary definition, printed p. 31</a>.</p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 1: Parts, limits, and rubric overview", "url": "#cycle/c1/limits", "html": "<section id=\"limits\"><h2>What you will submit</h2><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Part, Submission, Maximum length\"><table class=\"c1-limits\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Submission</th><th scope=\"col\">Maximum length</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Data tables and equity-gap analysis</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages of responses</strong>, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Contributing factors and problem statement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Strategies, feedback, adjustments, and partner involvement</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 pages</strong></td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Submission\">Reflective narrative</td><td data-label=\"Maximum length\"><strong>5 written pages OR 5 minutes of video explanation</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>These are limits for each complete part, not for each question. Submit one file per part. Written files may be DOCX, ODT, or PDF. Use the original templates with Arial 11-point type, single spacing, and one-inch margins. The original template text and your responses count toward the applicable limit, with the stated exclusion for Part A's data tables. The guide does not give charts a separate page-count exemption. Excess pages are not read or scored.</p>\n<p>First develop the evidence and reasoning each question needs; then revise for clarity and length. The teaching explanations and annotated examples on this page are longer than a candidate response. They are not a completed, page-fitted submission to copy. The Part D video option addresses the same three reflection prompts; it is not a recording of a meeting.</p>\n<p class=\"source-note\">CTC Cycle 1 Version 09: <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=26\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">preparing evidence, printed pp. 22-23</a>; <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=28\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer\">submission specifications, printed pp. 24-25</a>. Part D permits asf, qt, mov, mpg, mpeg, avi, wmv, mp4, or m4v video files.</p></section><section id=\"rubric-map\"><h2>How the parts and rubrics fit together</h2><p>A rubric describes what assessors examine in your evidence and explanation. Read it as a guide to the reasoning you need to make visible. This overview introduces the eight rubrics; the complete five-level descriptors appear with the relevant parts below.</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Step, Part, Purpose, Rubric focus\"><table class=\"c1-overview-map\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Step</th><th scope=\"col\">Part</th><th scope=\"col\">Purpose</th><th scope=\"col\">Rubric focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Investigate</td><td data-label=\"Part\">A</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Identify an equity gap.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\"><strong>1.1</strong></a>: Analyze three years of numerical data and justify the selected group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\"><strong>1.2</strong></a>: Analyze three qualitative sources and connect findings to the numbers.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\"><strong>1.3</strong></a>: Explain the equity gap and its relationship to school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Plan</td><td data-label=\"Part\">B</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Examine factors and define the problem.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\"><strong>1.4</strong></a>: Support potential contributing factors with evidence and research.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\"><strong>1.5</strong></a>: Develop a focused, feasible problem responsive to the group's needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Act</td><td data-label=\"Part\">C</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Propose and refine strategies.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\"><strong>1.6</strong></a>: Connect strategies to findings, factors, the problem, and school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\"></td><td data-label=\"Part\"></td><td data-label=\"Purpose\"></td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\"><strong>1.7</strong></a>: Apply feedback, build partner support, and anticipate implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Step\">Reflect</td><td data-label=\"Part\">D</td><td data-label=\"Purpose\">Examine leadership learning.</td><td data-label=\"Rubric focus\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\"><strong>1.8</strong></a>: Explain evidence-supported leadership strengths and growth.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Investigate:</strong> Examine an attendance disparity alongside students' experiences and access to support.</li><li><strong>Plan:</strong> Examine whether unclear referral responsibilities interfere with timely assistance.</li><li><strong>Act:</strong> Seek feedback on a proposed process and adjust it for accessibility and staffing.</li><li><strong>Reflect:</strong> Explain how investigating access changed your understanding and identified a learning need.</li></ul>\n<p>Several responses and tables may contribute to the same rubric. The rubric evaluates their combined evidence and reasoning; one phrase or example does not establish a score. In the complete rubrics, <strong>OR</strong> identifies alternative shortcomings, while <strong>All of Level 3, plus</strong> and <strong>All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus</strong> mean that higher levels retain the earlier requirements.</p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 1 Part A", "url": "#cycle/c1/part-a", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-a\">Part A - Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages of responses, excluding quantitative and qualitative data tables. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.1, 1.2, 1.3.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S1_A_DataCollectionEquityGapAnalysis.docx\" download>Download the original Part A template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part A brings together numerical data, qualitative evidence, and the school's vision, mission, or goals. Its purpose is to develop a supported explanation of an equity concern. You first organize the evidence in Sections I-III, then explain your decisions and analysis in Section IV.</p><p>The first template page shows the quantitative table and student-group selection, followed by the qualitative table. The second page begins with school commitments and continues to the five analysis questions. The pictures retain the original layout; the exact numbered prompts also appear in readable text beside the guidance below.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 1 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part A template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<p>Part A has four sections: I. Initial Data Collection; II. Extended Data Collection; III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals; IV. Equity Gap Analysis. Begin with the evidence tables, then explain the decisions and relationships in the written responses.</p>\n<section><h3>Read the numbers before choosing an explanation</h3><p>Quantitative data are numerical observations: counts, percentages, rates, or scores. Check what is being counted and who is included. The <strong>numerator</strong> is the count meeting the condition; the <strong>denominator</strong> is the relevant population used to calculate the rate. In the latest fictional year, 30 chronically absent students divided by 100 eligible students gives 30%. A count of enrolled students may differ from the denominator used in a particular report.</p><p>Read both change over time and differences within the same year. A schoolwide improvement may coexist with a persistent group disparity. The schoolwide rate includes the selected group; it is a descriptive reference, not an independent comparison group. Students can appear in more than one reporting category, so subgroup rows generally cannot be added together.</p><p>A blank or suppressed result is not zero. Retain the source's explanation, examine counts as well as rates, and do not reconstruct suppressed information. Keep the same metric and confirm historical definitions before comparing years. Three annual groups are not necessarily the same students followed over time.</p><p>The template lists common student groups and permits rows to be added or deleted as needed. Examine relevant available groups before selecting one. If you investigate a combined group, such as a particular grade within a language group, you need evidence for that actual combination; adding two separate subgroup rows cannot create it.</p></section><h3>I. Initial Data Collection</h3>\n<p>The original table puts the most recent year first. The template permits adding or deleting student-group rows; the table below is a filled instructional selection, not a complete school extract. Each cell gives the rate and count / eligible students. Additional groups in a real school should be examined as relevant; missing or suppressed data must not be recorded as zero.</p>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Student group</th><th scope=\"col\">2025-26: most recent fictional year</th><th scope=\"col\">2024-25</th><th scope=\"col\">2023-24</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">All students</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">18% (90/500)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">20% (100/500)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">22% (110/500)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Students with disabilities</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">30% (30/100)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">32% (32/100)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">34% (34/100)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">English learners</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">20% (16/80)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">22.5% (18/80)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">25% (20/80)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Socioeconomically disadvantaged</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">22% (66/300)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">24% (72/300)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">26% (78/300)</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Student group\">Students without disabilities</td><td data-label=\"2025-26: most recent toy year\">15% (60/400)</td><td data-label=\"2024-25\">17% (68/400)</td><td data-label=\"2023-24\">19% (76/400)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>All-student counts include the selected group. English learner, disability, and socioeconomic categories overlap, so these rows cannot be summed. The without-disabilities row is the fictional complement of the disability row, not a separate official Dashboard field claimed here. Rates use the same fictional definition in each year, but the student membership can change.</p>\n<h3>Three views modeled on Equity Gap</h3>\n<p>The saved Equity Gap report includes available-year trends, same-year school/district/state comparisons, and subgroup gaps. These charts mirror those analytical views using fictional data. They are newly plotted illustrations, not screenshots or exports from the app. <a href=\"sources/010_full_report_jordan_high.txt\">Compare these chart types with the saved app report</a>.</p>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/attendance-trend.png\" alt=\"Fictional attendance trend: all students 22, 20, 18 percent; students with disabilities 34, 32, 30 percent. Gap stays 12 points.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"896\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1. Both groups improve four percentage points, but the difference remains 12 points.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/system-comparison.png\" alt=\"Fictional latest-year chronic absence: school 18 percent, district 20 percent, state 22 percent.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"896\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1-T2. A favorable schoolwide comparison can conceal a persistent within-school gap. The fictional district and state are contextual references, not matched controls.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" title=\"Enlarge Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/charts/subgroup-gaps.png\" alt=\"Fictional gaps from schoolwide 18 percent: students with disabilities plus 12, socioeconomic disadvantage plus 4, English learners plus 2, students without disabilities minus 3 points.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1440\" height=\"928\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>T1. Positive values mean more chronic absenteeism than schoolwide. The chart identifies questions; it cannot identify causes.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Latest-year comparison</th><th scope=\"col\">Chronically absent / eligible</th><th scope=\"col\">Rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">School (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">90 / 500</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">18%</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">District (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">1,000 / 5,000</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">20%</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Latest-year comparison\">State (fictional)</td><td data-label=\"Chronically absent / eligible\">110,000 / 500,000</td><td data-label=\"Rate\">22%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><a href=\"sources/cycle1-fictional-attendance.csv\" download=\"\">Download the fictional counts, denominators, rates, and source labels</a>. The CSV is the numerical source for every chart. No real school data have been relabeled as fictional.</p>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q1\"><h3>I. Initial Data Collection - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tIdentify the one specific student group you have chosen to further investigate.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Name one group after reviewing the numerical pattern.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, the three-year table and trend chart.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I selected students with disabilities. Their chronic absenteeism rate exceeds the schoolwide rate by 12 percentage points in all three fictional years. Both rates decline by four points, so overall improvement has not narrowed this difference. This is a reason to investigate access and support.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The choice follows the evidence and keeps one group in view. Disability status is a reporting category, not a causal explanation.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Choosing a group first and searching only for numbers that confirm an assumption.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1</a>.</p></section>\n<section><h3>Use each source to answer a specific question</h3><p>Qualitative evidence helps you understand experiences, processes, and context. It can include interview accounts, observations, and analysis of school documents. Decide what you need to understand before selecting the sources. Three quotations from the same interview do not create three different sources.</p><p>Ask about concrete experiences: what happened when a student or family tried to obtain support, what response they received, and what helped or hindered access. During observations, record what you see and hear before adding an interpretation. For document analysis, examine how a policy, schedule, procedure, or record describes responsibilities and actual follow-through.</p><p>Analyze the evidence by comparing recurring themes, differences, and counterexamples. Explain how you selected participants or records, whose perspectives may be missing, and how access or language needs shaped participation. Counts of comments can describe your small sample, but they do not estimate how common an experience is across the entire school.</p><p>Follow your program and site procedures when collecting evidence. Keep identifiable records in authorized storage and protect identities in submitted materials. The school's required permissions and the candidates' own evidence cannot be supplied by a fictional example.</p></section><h3>II. Extended Data Collection</h3>\n<p>The original qualitative table has two columns: Data Sources and Qualitative Data. Record observations or accounts and then analyze their meaning. Interview themes, observed processes, and document content supply different evidence.</p>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Data Sources</th><th scope=\"col\">Qualitative Data</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q1. Eight volunteer student-caregiver interviews. Fictional notes; prompts ask about a recent attempt to obtain attendance support.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">Six accounts describe unclear contacts or arrangements they could not use; two describe helpful direct coordination. Example invented excerpts: \"I was told to call the other office, but did not know whom to ask for\" and \"Our contact called when I could answer and arranged the next step.\" These are contrasting experiences, not prevalence estimates.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q2. Three morning arrival observations. Fictional field notes from the school office.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">On two mornings, an arriving family was redirected between offices before the responsible adult was identified. On the third, a named staff member made a direct handoff. The notes document process variation; they do not explain annual absence or establish disability status by appearance. The teaching case stipulates that these encounters concern the selected group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Data Sources\">Q3. Document analysis of G1, the referral procedure, and six selected referral records for the group. Fictional records.</td><td data-label=\"Qualitative Data\">The plan allocates follow-up time and names response deadlines. The procedure has a referral step but no explicit owner for confirming completion. Four records lack documented completion; two name an adult and record a completed arrangement. Missing documentation leaves delivery uncertain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A real inquiry would plan accessible participation and collect viewpoints beyond volunteers where appropriate. The examples above are a small teaching packet. More interviews or observations could challenge the interpretation.</p>\n<figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-A-2.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part A template, page 2 of 2. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part A template, page 2. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure><section><h3>III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals</h3><p>Locate the actual school commitment relevant to your indicator and student group. A plan, mission statement, or improvement goal can establish the intended outcome and resources, but its presence does not demonstrate that the promised service occurred. Record the document and relevant language, then explain the connection to your inquiry.</p><p>The fictional excerpt below is specific enough to examine: it identifies a priority group, an allocation of staff time, and response commitments. Your real school's wording may be different. Use its verified commitments rather than inventing a goal that happens to match your preferred strategy.</p></section><h3>G1 - Fictional school commitment and allocation</h3>\n<blockquote><p>The school improvement plan identifies students with disabilities as a priority group for reducing chronic absenteeism. It allocates two weekly staff periods to attendance follow-up. A referred family should have a named contact within two school days and an agreed accessible support arrangement within five school days.</p></blockquote>\n<p>The two-day and five-day targets are invented school commitments, not CalAPA rules. Compare G1 with Q3: an allocation and a promise do not establish that families received usable support.</p>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q2\"><h3>III. School Vision, Mission, and/or Goals - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tInvestigate the vision, mission, and/or goals at your school and document your findings.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Document a specific school commitment and its associated resources.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> G1, the fictional improvement-plan excerpt below.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The fictional school plan commits to reducing chronic absenteeism for students with disabilities and providing accessible attendance support. It assigns two weekly staff periods for family follow-up, a named contact within two school days of referral, and an agreed support arrangement within five school days. The concern is whether these allocated supports reach the intended students.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The stated goal becomes something the analysis can examine: allocation, access, monitoring, and outcome.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Inventing a real school goal or claiming that a plan proves services were delivered.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section>\n<p class=\"note\">The original Part A IV.1 prompt refers to quantitative data in Section II, although the quantitative table is in Section I. The original wording is retained below and in the images.</p>\n<section><h3>IV. Explain the decisions behind the evidence</h3><p>The five questions in Section IV ask you to explain how you moved from collected information to an equity-gap analysis. Refer to the tables, interpret the relationships, and make clear why the evidence matters. You can cite a finding again when it serves a different purpose, but avoid repeating the whole table in every response.</p><p>Analysis goes beyond description. \"The group rate is 30%\" describes a value. Explaining that both rates improved while their difference remained 12 percentage points identifies a relationship. Connecting that relationship with evidence about access and school commitments develops the equity analysis.</p><p>A percentage-point difference is found by subtraction: 30% minus 18% equals 12 percentage points. A percent change answers a different question and uses a denominator. Name the comparison explicitly so the reader does not confuse improvement over time with the difference between groups.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q3\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tReflecting upon the data collected and analyzed, what supports your decision to select this specific equity gap for your identified group of students? Share your analysis for the specific quantitative data you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template and your rationale for collecting it. How are these data relevant to understanding equity issues at the school for student groups?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain the three-year pattern, why you collected these data, and why the group warrants investigation.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1-T2 and all three charts.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Schoolwide chronic absenteeism declined from 22% (110/500) to 20% (100/500) to 18% (90/500). For students with disabilities it declined from 34% (34/100) to 32% (32/100) to 30% (30/100). Each declined four percentage points; the gap remained 12 points. I used the same indicator across three years to examine persistence. The latest schoolwide rate is below the fictional district and state rates, but that favorable comparison does not resolve the within-school disparity. The rates describe annual groups, not the same children followed over time.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It interprets improvement and persistent disparity together. Numerators, denominators, years, and comparison choices remain visible.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Writing \"12 percent higher\" when the intended comparison is 12 percentage points. The relative difference in the latest year is a different calculation.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-1\">Rubric 1.1</a>.</p></section><section id=\"qualitative-analysis\"><h3>From a record to a bounded finding</h3><p>This is one possible way to analyze qualitative evidence, not a required coding procedure, sample size, software package, or additional upload. All excerpts and observations are invented within the Cedar Grove case.</p><p><strong>Record:</strong> one of the Q1 accounts says, \"I was told to call the other office, but did not know whom to ask for.\" Preserve the account as a report of that person's experience. A tentative category is <em>unclear handoff responsibility</em>; avoid the unsupported label \"family disengagement.\"</p><p><strong>Compare:</strong> another Q1 account describes a contact calling at a usable time and arranging the next step. Q2 likewise includes a direct handoff on one of three observed mornings. These contrasting cases challenge a claim that support was always inaccessible.</p><p><strong>Check another source:</strong> Q3 finds four of six selected records without documented completion and two with it. Missing documentation leaves delivery uncertain; it does not prove no help occurred.</p><p><strong>Bounded finding:</strong> coordination appears inconsistent in this small selected packet. The evidence warrants examining how the promised support reaches students with disabilities. It cannot estimate schoolwide prevalence or explain how much of the 12-point attendance disparity the process caused. Read this alongside G1's resource and response commitments in the equity-gap synthesis.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q4\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tDescribe the three different qualitative data sources you included in Section II of the Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis template. How did these sources provide more information about this student group for the California state indicator?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Describe three different qualitative sources and what each contributes.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1 interviews, Q2 observations, Q3 document analysis.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I compared eight volunteer student-caregiver interviews, three morning arrival observations, and a review of the attendance plan, referral procedure, and six referral records. Interviews showed how support was experienced; observations showed how arrival handoffs operated; documents showed intended responsibilities and recorded follow-through. These sources were selected to examine how attendance supports reached students with disabilities. They are small purposeful samples, not representative estimates.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The sources have different jobs and address the same selected group and indicator.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Counting three quotations from one interview as three different sources.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q5\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tWhat patterns and/or trends related to the California state indicator and student group equity issues did you find in the qualitative data? How do they relate to the quantitative data patterns and/or trends you identified?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Compare patterns across sources and explain their relationship to the numbers.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3, including the successful support cases.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Six interview accounts described difficulty identifying a contact or arranging a usable support time. Two described successful direct help. On two observed mornings, staff redirected a family between offices before identifying the responsible adult; the third morning included a direct handoff. Four reviewed referral records lacked documented follow-through; two included it. These findings suggest inconsistent access and monitoring alongside a persistent attendance disparity. Missing documentation does not prove that no help occurred, and an arrival delay is not itself a chronically absent classification.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Convergence and counterexamples narrow the claim. Qualitative events are not incorrectly treated as the annual outcome measure.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Turning six volunteer accounts into \"75% of all families\" or saying the observations prove why students were absent.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-2\">Rubric 1.2</a>, <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q6\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tDefine the equity gap you identified for a student group through your quantitative and qualitative data analyses. Discuss related research and explain how this research informs and/or supports your equity gap analysis finding for the California state indicator and student group you identified.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Define the equity gap by connecting outcomes, school resources, access, and relevant research.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, and the research connection.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The equity concern is a persistent 12-point chronic-absence disparity for students with disabilities alongside inconsistent access to the follow-up support promised in the school plan. Staff time and response commitments are allocated, but the reviewed experiences and records do not show reliable access or monitoring. The attendance-messaging study by <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a> supports examining communication as one potentially modifiable process. It does not establish the cause of this disparity or the effect of this proposed referral procedure. Health needs, transportation, and attendance-record accuracy remain questions for additional inquiry.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The definition includes the mismatch between intended support and experienced access. The research has a stated use and a stated limit.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Defining equity only as a subtraction, or calling the proposed access mechanism a proven cause.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p><p><a href=\"#cycle/c1/b-q3\">Continue to the revised research connection in Part B, question 3</a>, which tests and narrows this initial interpretation.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"a-q7\"><h3>IV. Equity Gap Analysis - Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tHow is the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school related to specific components of the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain the exact connection to the school commitment documented in Section III.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, G1, Q1, and Q3.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The fictional plan explicitly names students with disabilities in its attendance goal and provides staff time and response deadlines for accessible support. The 30% latest-year rate and 12-point disparity show the outcome concern. Incomplete follow-through records and mixed family experiences raise a question about whether the allocation is fulfilling that commitment. I would examine actual delivery and accessibility before concluding that the school simply needs more services.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It connects the school promise to evidence of delivery and outcomes.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Saying only \"this aligns with our mission of success for all.\"</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-3\">Rubric 1.3</a>.</p></section>\n<h3>Research connection and its boundary</h3><p>The IES randomized study of approximately 26,000 K-5 students found that adaptive parent messaging reduced chronic absence without improving achievement in the study year (<a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a>). This supports investigating communication and response processes. It does not identify the cause of Cedar Grove's disparity or establish effects specifically for students with disabilities.</p>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-1\"><h3>Rubric 1.1</h3><p>Essential Question: Based on the chosen California state indicator, how well does the candidate select and analyze quantitative data sources across the three most recent years, identify patterns and/or trends related to equity, and choose an appropriate student group?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not include quantitative data across the three most recent years. OR Patterns and trends are not identified or are irrelevant. OR Candidate does not select a student group to investigate OR selects a student group not appropriate for the identified indicator.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Essential evidence is absent or irrelevant; the OR conditions are alternatives.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects a California state indicator* and minimally analyzes quantitative data, vaguely identifying patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate is not clear about which student group they will investigate or why the student group was selected.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Some numerical analysis is present, but the pattern or choice of group remains unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate selects a California state indicator* and analyzes quantitative data across the three most recent years, clearly identifying general patterns and/or trends related to school equity. Candidate clearly identifies a student group to investigate further and provides a rationale for why this student group was selected.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Analyze three years, identify an equity pattern, and justify one group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explores additional data linked to the indicator that clearly informs their understanding of patterns and/or trends to further clarify group differences within the state indicator selected (e.g., demographic data, ethnicity, gender, language).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Add relevant data that actually clarify within-indicator group differences.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant research that informs their understanding (patterns and/or trends related to equity) of the determined equity issue for the chosen student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use relevant research to inform the deeper quantitative interpretation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Note: *California state indicator: Chronic Absenteeism, Suspension Rate, English Learner Progress, Graduation Rate, Academic Performance, College/Career Readiness Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 10. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=14\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The three-year table and A IV.1 explain the persistent disparity and group selection. The system comparison adds context, but another chart alone does not demonstrate a higher level. Relevant research must inform the interpretation, not simply appear in a citation.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-2\"><h3>Rubric 1.2</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate collect and analyze relevant qualitative data from a range of at least three different qualitative* data sources and explain their relationship of the findings from the analysis to the quantitative** data findings and the identified student group equity issue?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not provide or analyze three qualitative* data sources. OR Candidate provides no or irrelevant information about the connection among qualitative and quantitative data** findings and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Three sources are missing or unanalyzed, or the connection to the equity issue is missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate collects and minimally analyzes a range of qualitative* data from at least three sources. Candidate minimally connects the qualitative data and findings to the quantitative data** findings for the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Sources and connections receive only minimal analysis.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate collects and analyzes a range of relevant qualitative* data from three different sources. Candidate clearly explains the relationship among the findings from the analyses of the qualitative data, the quantitative data,** and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Analyze three different relevant sources and explain how findings relate to the numbers and group.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate collects additional qualitative data as appropriate to deepen their understanding of the California state indicator chosen and the student group equity issue, and provides a complete analysis of the relationship between their qualitative and quantitative data findings and the identified student group equity issue.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Gather additional appropriate qualitative evidence and analyze how it deepens the connection.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate explains how their qualitative data collection strategy is responsive to the complex context in which they are working and demonstrates cultural sensitivity and an appreciation for diverse viewpoints.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how collection responds to context, cultural sensitivity, and diverse viewpoints.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Notes: *Qualitative: Collected through methods like interviews, focus groups, and observations, this data is subjective, rich in context, and commonly analyzed by identifying themes, patterns, or through coding (e.g., responses from interviews with students, faculty, or families/guardians; notes from observations; document analysis) **Quantitative: Numerical data expressing a certain quantity, amount, or range. Usually, there are measurement units associated with the data (e.g., meters, degrees, score points). Arithmetic and statistical operations may be applied to quantitative data. Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Element 3B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 11. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=15\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: Q1-Q3 provide three kinds of evidence and preserve successful as well as difficult experiences. The example does not claim to demonstrate the additional collection required at Level 4 or a complete culturally responsive collection strategy at Level 5.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-3\"><h3>Rubric 1.3</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate conduct an equity gap analysis based on the chosen California state indicator to inform their understanding of the equity issues for the identified student group? How does this equity gap align with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue with no evidence of quantitative or qualitative data analysis. OR Candidate does not identify any patterns and/or trends in the equity gap analysis provided. OR Candidate does not provide a description of an equity gap for the student group. OR Candidate does not identify the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The equity issue lacks necessary evidence, pattern, description, or school-goal alignment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group based on minimal quantitative or qualitative data analysis. Candidate identifies patterns and/or trends that are not clear in the equity gap analysis provided or does not clearly describe the identified patterns and/or trends. Candidate provides a minimal description of an equity gap for the student group. Candidate minimally describes the alignment between the equity gap for a student group at the school and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The analysis, pattern, definition, or alignment is only minimally developed.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies an equity issue for an identified student group clearly based on their quantitative and qualitative data analysis for the chosen state indicator. Candidate clearly describes patterns and/or trends across the quantitative and qualitative data analysis in the equity gap analysis provided. Candidate clearly identifies the alignment between the equity gap for the identified student group and the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Integrate quantitative and qualitative findings and align the issue to a specific school commitment.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate conducts a thorough equity gap analysis, describing a clear connection from quantitative data findings to supportive qualitative data findings, and provides a sophisticated understanding of the equity disparity identified for the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain the relationships thoroughly to show a deeper understanding of the disparity.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites and explains how research informs their understanding of the equity gap for the specific student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how research informs understanding of this group's equity gap.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part A: Data Tables and Written Narrative: Data Collection and Equity Gap Analysis (up to 5 pages of responses to prompts, exclusive of quantitative and qualitative data tables) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 3; Elements 3B, 3C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 12. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=16\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: G1 connects resources and school commitments to the combined findings. The research connection has a stated limitation for the selected group; it does not establish the cause of this particular disparity.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Can you justify your selected group using the three-year pattern?</li><li>Have you analyzed three different qualitative sources and connected them to the numerical evidence?</li><li>Does the equity-gap explanation address resources, access, school commitments, and outcomes, including uncertainty?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-investigate/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: investigate</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-b\">Continue to Part B <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 1 Part B", "url": "#cycle/c1/part-b", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-b\">Part B - Contributing Factors and Problem Statement</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.4 and 1.5. Use evidence from Step 1 in each response. Embed research citations in the responses; the official instructions say not to provide a separate citation list.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S2_B_ContribFactorsAndProbStatement.docx\" download>Download the original Part B template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part B develops the explanation begun in Part A. A <strong>contributing factor</strong> is a condition that may help explain the identified equity gap. It must be supported by evidence and relevant research. Student identity is not, by itself, an explanation.</p><p><strong>Institutional factors</strong> can include established norms or practices that shape opportunities. <strong>Structural factors</strong> concern how responsibilities, schedules, decisions, and resources are organized. A referral procedure with no clear follow-up owner is a possible structural condition. A longstanding assumption that every family can attend a daytime appointment may be an institutional practice worth examining. These categories can overlap; explain how the actual condition operates rather than concentrating on its label.</p><p>Begin by comparing plausible explanations and checking them against Part A, including evidence that challenges your first interpretation. If the evidence does not clarify contributing factors, the manual directs you to collect more data or review relevant research. Then identify a need the school could address and develop one feasible problem statement. An <strong>educational need</strong> describes what students need to access or achieve; a <strong>strategy</strong> describes a proposed action to meet it.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-B-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part B template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part B template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q1\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tWhat potential contributing factors are suggested by the data you have collected and analyzed that may have created or added to the equity gap you identified for a student group at your school?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Identify potential school-controlled conditions supported by Part A.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3 and the completed Part A analysis.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Two potential factors are unclear ownership of attendance-support referrals and support arrangements that families cannot readily use. Q1 accounts and Q2 office handoffs point to unclear contacts; Q3 shows missing follow-through documentation. The successful cases suggest that direct adult coordination is already possible. Health and transportation may also matter, but these sources do not establish their contribution to the annual disparity.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The proposed factors are institutional processes supported by multiple sources.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Treating disability, family values, or a preferred intervention as the contributing factor.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q2\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tHow do these specific contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, impact student learning or well-being for the student group?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain a plausible mechanism connecting each condition to learning or well-being.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1 experience excerpts and Q2 observation notes.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>When responsibility is unclear, families may need to repeat requests before reaching an adult who can arrange help. A meeting time or contact format that a family cannot use may leave an identified barrier unresolved. These processes could prolong difficulty attending and reduce access to instruction. The observed office delays demonstrate friction in the support process, but they do not measure how many days of absence that friction produced.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> It explains a mechanism and keeps the size of the effect uncertain.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Claiming that four incomplete records caused the 12-point gap.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q3\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tCite research related to your findings regarding contributing factors that may have created or added to the equity gap you have identified for the student group.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Use research to examine the proposed mechanism and its limits.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The IES research connection introduced in Part A.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Initial draft: an incomplete research connection</p><p>The study by <a href=\"https://ies.ed.gov/use-work/resource-library/report/evaluation-report/can-texting-parents-improve-attendance-elementary-school-test-adaptive-messaging-strategy\">Heppen, Kurki, and Brown (2020), <i>Can Texting Parents Improve Attendance in Elementary School?</i>, NCEE 2020-006</a> gives a reason to examine timely, usable family communication as one potentially modifiable condition. Its evidence does not establish the effectiveness of a referral-handoff procedure for students with disabilities. I therefore treat accessible communication and documented follow-through as proposals requiring local feasibility review, while seeking more directly matched research on disability-related attendance barriers.</p></blockquote><p>What still needs revision: the draft acknowledges a limitation but has not yet used research to examine the selected group's access needs. The next passage demonstrates that revision.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Adding an unrelated citation or implying that a general K-5 finding establishes a disability-specific effect.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>.</p><h4>Revised research connection</h4><blockquote><p><a href=\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10803-023-06025-3\">Totsika and colleagues (2024; first published online in 2023)</a> studied parent-reported attendance among UK children aged 5-15 with intellectual disability and/or autism. Unmet school needs and the parent-teacher relationship were relevant to attendance. This broadens my question from whether families receive reminders to whether the offered support fits the child and can be used. The survey is observational, pandemic-period, and narrower than our full disability reporting group; it does not establish Cedar Grove's causes or validate a referral procedure.</p><p>I therefore interpret Q1-Q3 as evidence of inconsistent coordination, not proof that disability or family motivation explains absence. The successful direct-contact cases suggest a process worth examining. The IES messaging study supports attention to communication; it does not establish that messaging alone addresses access. My proposed handoff would confirm a usable arrangement and coordinate existing supports. Health, transport, individual needs, and the effect on attendance remain to be investigated.</p></blockquote><p><strong>What changed:</strong> the first draft relied on a general communication study. The revision uses a more relevant population to reconsider the mechanism, then limits the claim to what the local records support. CalAPA does not require every study to sample only the selected group; explain relevance and transfer limits.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q4\"><h3>I. Institutional/Structural Factors - Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tIdentify areas of educational need related to the single equity gap for a group of students (e.g., the school needs additional supports or materials, necessary specialists or counselors, after-school programs for students).</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> State the educational need before selecting a program.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Q1-Q3 and G1.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Students with disabilities and their families need a dependable, accessible route from an attendance concern to a named adult and a usable support arrangement. Staff need clear handoff responsibilities and a way to verify that the intended support was received. This need can be addressed within the school attendance process while individual health, service, and transportation needs are examined with the appropriate staff.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The need is connected to resources and access, with a feasible school-level scope.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Listing new programs without showing what the students need from them.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-4\">Rubric 1.4</a>, <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"b-q5\"><h3>II. Problem Statement - Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tProblem Statement: Describe the equity gap that needs to be addressed at the chosen school for the California state indicator and the identified student group\u2019s area of need (achievement and/or well-being).</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Write one focused problem statement that follows from the investigation.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> Part A, the potential factors, and the selected educational need.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>At fictional Cedar Grove Elementary, students with disabilities experience a persistent chronic-absence disparity: 30% compared with 18% schoolwide in the latest fictional year. Despite allocated follow-up time, reviewed family experiences, office handoffs, and referral records show inconsistent access to a named adult and confirmed support. The school needs a reliable process for arranging and checking accessible attendance support for this group. The initial improvement scope is the referral and follow-through process; the evidence does not explain every absence.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The statement names the group, outcome concern, access need, and feasible scope. Existing staff time and successful handoffs make this a plausible process to examine.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Writing \"buy an attendance app\" as the problem or promising to eliminate all absences.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-5\">Rubric 1.5</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-4\"><h3>Rubric 1.4</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to the identified equity gap affecting a student group and cite the research supporting their determination?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies contributing factors that are biased, superficial, or irrelevant to the equity gap analysis. OR Candidate does not cite research and/or neglects to draw connections between research and contributing factors.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Factors are biased, superficial, or irrelevant, or research is absent or disconnected.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate identifies potential contributing factors and minimally describes how they relate to the equity gap analysis. Candidate attempts to draw connections between research and contributing factors, but citations are not related to the equity gap.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Connections between factors, findings, and research remain weak.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly uses the equity gap analysis and findings from the quantitative and qualitative data analyses to determine contributing factors, including institutional and/or structural factors, that created or added to an equity gap affecting a student group. Candidate cites relevant research to support potential contributing factors that create or add to the identified equity gap affecting a student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use both kinds of evidence and relevant research to support institutional or structural factors.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains in detail (with supporting evidence from Step 1) how several contributing factors can create or add to equity differences or disparities for a student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain in detail, with Step 1 evidence, how several factors may contribute.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate demonstrates a sophisticated, research-based understanding of the systemic, institutional, or structural causes of the identified single equity gap for a group of students at the school.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Demonstrate a sophisticated research-based understanding of systemic or structural causes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 15. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=19\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The example identifies ownership and access conditions supported by Q1-Q3. The revised Part B research connection uses attendance and neurodevelopmental-condition studies to refine the inquiry while distinguishing local evidence, observational associations, and limits of transfer.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-5\"><h3>Rubric 1.5</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate use the equity gap analysis and identification of potential contributing factors to develop a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not use the equity gap analysis or potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement. OR Candidate\u2019s problem statement is not responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The problem does not follow from the investigation or does not respond to the group's needs.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate attempts to use the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors to develop a problem statement, but it is not clear how the problem statement supports student achievement and/or well-being of the student group. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is only partially responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Its connection to achievement or well-being, or its responsiveness, is unclear.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate develops a feasible problem statement related to student achievement and/or well-being of the student group that clearly draws from the equity gap analysis and potential contributing factors identified. Candidate\u2019s problem statement is clearly responsive to the needs of the student group.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Develop a feasible, responsive problem statement grounded in the analysis and factors.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate explains why their problem statement is responsive and feasible for the school\u2019s culture and context.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain feasibility and responsiveness in the school's culture and context.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate cites relevant evidence-based practices or research on how the area of educational need has been addressed in other school settings to improve achievement and/or well-being for similar student groups.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use evidence-based practice or research from other settings with similar student groups.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part B: Written Narrative: Contributing Factors and Problem Statement (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1A, 1C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 16. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=20\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The problem statement identifies a need the school can examine within existing staff time. Evidence from other settings with similar student groups would need to be evaluated before claiming the additional research connection described at Level 5.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does each potential factor have a clear connection to Step 1 evidence and relevant research?</li><li>Have you considered another explanation and contradictory evidence?</li><li>Does the problem statement identify a responsive, feasible school-level need?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-plan/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: plan</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-c\">Continue to Part C <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 1 Part C", "url": "#cycle/c1/part-c", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-c\">Part C - Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 pages. <b>Rubrics:</b> 1.6 and 1.7. Develop strategies, obtain targeted leader feedback, explain adjustments, and plan for buy-in and implications.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S3_C_PlanSchoolImprovementPromotEquity.docx\" download>Download the original Part C template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part C turns the problem statement into proposed action. A <strong>strategy</strong> explains what adults would do, how it responds to the investigated need, and what would make it feasible in this school. You need a clear connection between the evidence, the contributing conditions, and each proposed action.</p><p>Cycle 1's Act step includes obtaining targeted feedback from an administrator, supervisor, or educational leader familiar with the school. Arrange that conversation after you have a proposal specific enough to discuss. Ask about each strategy's appropriateness, feasibility, needed partners, and resources. Record the substance of the feedback and explain how you would adjust or strengthen the proposal. A plan to ask for feedback later does not supply evidence that you received it.</p><p><strong>Educational partners</strong> are the people whose knowledge, participation, or decisions matter to the work, including staff, students, families, and relevant community partners. <strong>Buy-in</strong> develops when people understand the proposal and have a meaningful chance to question and shape it. Explain that process and anticipate practical effects such as workload, scheduling, accessibility, and coordination. The fictional records below illustrate these decisions; the actual feedback in your submission must come from your own work.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-C-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part C template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part C template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<h3>F1 - Fictional feedback and proposed adjustments</h3>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Initial proposal</th><th scope=\"col\">Fictional targeted feedback</th><th scope=\"col\">Proposed adjustment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Send attendance reminders and mark them sent.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Principal: delivery does not establish that a family has usable support.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Confirm a named contact, preferred communication, and an agreed arrangement; record alternate attempts if contact is not made.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Introduce the process schoolwide at once.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Principal: the two staff periods may not cover the workload.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Pilot in one grade band and review staff capacity weekly.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Initial proposal\">Schedule another attendance meeting for each referral.</td><td data-label=\"Fictional targeted feedback\">Special education lead: another meeting may duplicate existing support and add an access burden.</td><td data-label=\"Proposed adjustment\">Check existing arrangements and coordinate before requesting another meeting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Proposed evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Question it could answer</th><th scope=\"col\">What it would not establish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Referral and follow-through log</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Was a responsible adult assigned, and was support arranged?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">That the arrangement caused better attendance.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Family confirmation and preferred-format feedback</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Could the family use the arrangement?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">That all families experienced it the same way.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Workload review and unresolved-barrier notes</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">Is the process feasible, and what remains unresolved?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">A schoolwide effect after a short pilot.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed evidence\">Later attendance records with denominators</td><td data-label=\"Question it could answer\">How do absence patterns develop?</td><td data-label=\"What it would not establish\">Causation from a before/after comparison alone.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section id=\"strategy-map\"><h3>Proposed action and evidence map</h3><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Proposed action, Local evidence, Intended access change, Feedback adjustment, Still untested\"><table class=\"\"><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Proposed action</th><th scope=\"col\">Local evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Intended access change</th><th scope=\"col\">Feedback adjustment</th><th scope=\"col\">Still untested</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Name a handoff owner</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">Q1 unclear contacts; Q2 redirections; Q3 incomplete records</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">A family reaches an adult who can arrange help</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: confirm receipt and a usable arrangement, not only a sent message</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Whether support is received consistently or attendance improves</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Agree on usable contact and support arrangements</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">Q1 contrasting experiences; G1 accessible-support commitment</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">Contact timing and format fit the family and student</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: coordinate existing supports before adding meetings</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Which arrangements fit individual needs</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Proposed action\">Review follow-through during allocated staff time</td><td data-label=\"Local evidence\">G1 allocation; four incomplete Q3 records</td><td data-label=\"Intended access change\">Unresolved referrals receive attention</td><td data-label=\"Feedback adjustment\">F1: start with one grade band and review workload</td><td data-label=\"Still untested\">Capacity, equitable reach, and any outcome effect</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This map organizes the fictional proposal and feedback for Part C questions 1-4. It is a teaching aid, not another required submission.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q1\"><h3>Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tFor the student equity gap you identified in Step 1, describe the potential strategies you identified for equitable school improvement and how they are to be applied.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Describe what adults would do, when, and with what resources.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The Part B problem and G1 staffing allocation.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I propose a named attendance contact for each referral, confirmation of the family's preferred language and accessible communication method, an agreed support appointment, and a documented check that the arrangement was usable. A designated staff member would review unresolved referrals during the two existing weekly follow-up periods. Appropriate personnel would examine transport, health, or service concerns when identified. A six-week pilot would examine feasibility and access.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The proposal identifies a process and resources. Its timeline is a proposed local decision, not a CalAPA requirement.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Announcing \"improve communication\" without assigning responsibility or checking access.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q2\"><h3>Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tExplain how your potential strategies address the equity gap described in your problem statement.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain how each proposed action responds to the problem.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, F1, and the <a href=\"#cycle/c1/strategy-map\">proposed action and evidence map</a>.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Naming an adult responds to repeated uncertainty about who could help. Agreeing on a usable contact method and appointment responds to access problems in the interviews. Recording the follow-through responds to the monitoring gap in four reviewed records. These changes aim to make the existing allocation reach the selected group. They do not guarantee a reduction in the annual chronic-absence rate.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Each action has a specific evidence-based rationale.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Choosing actions from a generic improvement menu without returning to Part A.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q3\"><h3>Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tExplain how your potential strategies for improvement address or take into account the potential contributing factors\u2014including institutional and/or structural factors\u2014and how they align with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Connect strategies to contributing factors and school commitments.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> G1, B1-B4, and the proposed process.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>The proposal addresses unclear ownership and inconsistent support access by assigning the handoff and confirming whether it worked. It uses the two staff periods already allocated in G1 and checks the plan's two-day contact and five-day arrangement commitments. Family preferences and disability-related access needs would guide arrangements. Staff would examine whether workload allows these commitments before expanding the process.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The strategy addresses organizational conditions and the documented goal.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Assuming the existing schedule has spare capacity without checking it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-6\">Rubric 1.6</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q4\"><h3>Question 4</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>4.\tDescribe the targeted feedback you received on each proposed strategy from your administrator, supervisor, or other key educational leader(s), and explain how you would adjust your proposed strategies to address the needs of the student group and inform equitable school improvement based on that feedback.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Report actual targeted feedback in a real submission and explain proposed adjustments.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1, the explicitly fictional feedback record.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>In the fictional feedback conversation, the principal warned that a message-sent checkbox could be mistaken for accessible support. I would add family confirmation or a documented alternate contact attempt. The principal also asked whether two staff periods could cover the workload, so I would start with one grade band and review capacity weekly. The special education lead advised coordinating with existing support arrangements; I would add that check before proposing another meeting.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The example shows feedback on the proposed strategies and consequential adjustments. These conversations are invented teaching evidence.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Presenting a future request for feedback as completed feedback, or writing only \"the principal approved.\"</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"c-q5\"><h3>Question 5</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>5.\tDescribe steps you would take to create school-level and community educational partner buy-in to the final adjusted set of proposed strategies. What potential implications for proposed strategies do you anticipate encountering as you address the equity gap at the school?</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Plan meaningful partner involvement and anticipate practical consequences.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1 and the revised strategy.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Before a pilot, I would ask families and students to review contact wording and timing, ask office staff and teachers to test the handoff, and ask the special education lead to check access and coordination. I would record disagreements and revisions before agreeing on responsibilities. Anticipated issues include staff workload, inaccessible communication, duplicate contacts, and interpreting unresolved health barriers as noncompliance. A weekly review would examine access and workload before expanding the process.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> People can influence the proposal; the plan anticipates feasible implementation concerns.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Treating an announcement or signature as evidence of buy-in.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-7\">Rubric 1.7</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-6\"><h3>Rubric 1.6</h3><p>Essential Question: How are the strategy(ies) proposed for equitable school improvement for the student group well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis, including contributing factors, and responsive to the problem statement? How is the proposed strategy(ies) aligned to the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not propose strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement informed by the findings. OR Proposed strategy(ies) are not aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Strategies are not informed by the findings or aligned to the school's commitments.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) are minimally informed by the findings and provide only general reference to the equity gap analysis, contributing factors, and/or problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are partially aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The connection to findings and goals is general or partial.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate\u2019s proposed strategy(ies) for equitable school improvement for the student group are well informed by the findings of the equity gap analysis and the contributing factors are responsive to the problem statement. Proposed strategy(ies) are clearly aligned with the school\u2019s vision, mission, and/or goals.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Connect strategies clearly to the gap analysis, factors, problem, and school goals.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate provides relevant strategy(ies) that strategically focus on equitable student and school improvement and represent a contextually responsive approach to addressing the equity issue or educational need.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Show how the approach responds strategically to this particular context.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate provides research-based evidence of the relevance of the proposed strategy(ies) and their implementation for improving student achievement and/or well-being for the specific student group and school.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Provide research-based evidence for the strategy and implementation relevant to this group and school.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Element 1A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Element 5B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 18. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=22\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The proposed handoff responds to the evidence and G1 allocation. Its effect has not been evaluated in this fictional case; research about messaging does not validate the entire adapted procedure.</p></section><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-7\"><h3>Rubric 1.7</h3><p>Essential Question: How does the candidate apply the feedback received from administrators or educational leaders familiar with the school culture and context and describe next steps for creating educational partner buy-in and potential implications for the adjusted set of strategy(ies)?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not apply feedback from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen their proposed strategy(ies). OR Candidate states their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with little or no explanation of steps for buy-in from educational partner(s). OR Candidate does not identify anticipated implications for proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Feedback is not used, steps for buy-in are poorly explained, or implications are missing.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate vaguely describes feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader and makes irrelevant and/or inappropriate adjustments to proposed strategy(ies). Candidate vaguely describes their plan(s) to communicate the proposed strategy(ies) with educational partner(s); it is not clear that educational partner(s) will have the opportunity to develop buy-in. Candidate does not clearly describe anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Feedback, adjustments, partner involvement, or likely consequences remain vague.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly applies feedback received from an administrator/supervisor/educational leader to adjust or strengthen proposed strategy(ies). Candidate clearly provides relevant and appropriate next steps for creating buy-in and communicating with educational partner(s) to address the equity gap and learning need(s). Candidate clearly describes anticipated, realistic implications that may be encountered at the school related to the implementation of proposed strategy(ies).</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Apply relevant feedback, explain appropriate buy-in steps, and anticipate realistic implications.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate seeks additional rounds of feedback from other administrators or educational partners on the revised strategy(ies) and develops a detailed strategic plan to communicate and share the plan with a diverse range of educational partners to ensure they are proposing a workable/feasible approach to addressing the equity gap and learning need.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Seek further feedback on revisions and develop a detailed plan involving diverse partners.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate plans to coach educational partners to examine and address potential biases that could impact student learning and/or well-being due to identified equity gaps, including those specifically related to sources of education disadvantage or discrimination, and is transparent about the potential underlying contributing factors.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Plan to coach partners to examine biases and disadvantage while being transparent about factors.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part C: Written Narrative: Planning for School Improvement and Promoting Equity (up to 5 pages) CAPE Standard 1; Elements 1B, 1C CAPE Standard 2; Element 2A CAPE Standard 3; Element 3C CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5B, 5C CAPE Standard 6; Elements 6A, 6B</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 19. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=23\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: F1 makes the feedback and proposed adjustment visible. Proposed future partner conversations are not completed additional rounds of feedback. A Level 4 claim would require the further feedback described in that descriptor.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Have you explained the application and rationale for each proposed strategy?</li><li>Can you identify actual targeted leader feedback and the proposed adjustment it prompted?</li><li>Have you planned how partners can influence the proposal and anticipated realistic implications?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-act/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: act</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section><p class=\"c1-next\"><a href=\"#cycle/c1/part-d\">Continue to Part D <i data-lucide=\"arrow-right\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></a></p></section>"}, {"title": "Cycle 1 Part D", "url": "#cycle/c1/part-d", "html": "<section class=\"c1-part\"><h2 id=\"part-d\">Part D - Reflective Narrative</h2>\n<p><b>Limit:</b> 5 written pages OR up to 5 minutes of video explanation. <b>Rubric:</b> 1.8. The video option is your reflective explanation; Cycle 1 does not require the meeting clips used in later cycles.</p>\n<p><a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_S4_D_ReflectiveNarrative.docx\" download>Download the original Part D template</a></p><p class=\"source-note\">The example explains reasoning, not a score. Read each response with its related tables and complete rubrics; use your own evidence for every prompt.</p><p>Part D asks you to examine what the cycle taught you about your leadership. Refer to specific decisions and evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Explain your initial understanding, what challenged or strengthened it, and how it influenced your choices.</p><p>A useful reflection includes both a strength and an area for growth. Naming a limitation does not cancel your accomplishment; it helps explain what you have learned and what you need to practice next. Consider how the school's social, economic, or cultural context affected your approach and whose perspectives changed a decision.</p><p>Use the same three prompts whether you write or choose the video explanation. For video, plan your evidence references and check that all three prompts fit within five minutes; speak naturally about your own work. For either format, distinguish what you learned from what your proposed strategy might accomplish. A revised proposal is evidence of your reasoning, not evidence that the student outcome has already improved.</p><div class=\"template-pages\"><figure class=\"c1-figure c1-template\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"c1-image-expand\" data-figure-expand=\"\" data-image-title=\"Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" aria-label=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" title=\"Enlarge Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\"><img src=\"assets/cycle1/templates/part-D-1.png\" alt=\"Original Cycle 1 Part D template, page 1 of 1. CTC Version 09.\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"1275\" height=\"1650\"><span class=\"map-expand-icon\"><i data-lucide=\"expand\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></span></button><figcaption>Original Part D template, page 1. Rendered from the supplied Word file. Copyright 2026 California Commission on Teacher Credentialing. Full page and notices retained.</figcaption></figure></div>\n<section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q1\"><h3>Question 1</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>1.\tProvide your rationale for the administrator, supervisor, or educational leader feedback you chose to gather in Step 3 and how the feedback impacted your approach to building positive educational partner buy-in.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain why you sought that leader's feedback and how it changed your approach to buy-in.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> F1 and the before-and-after decisions in Part C.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>I sought the principal's feedback because she controls staff time and understands office responsibilities. The special education lead could test whether the process coordinated with existing supports and access needs. Their fictional feedback changed my initial emphasis on sending reminders to confirming usable support. It also changed buy-in from announcing a finished procedure to asking families and staff to test it before adoption.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The rationale explains the feedback providers' relevance and a specific change in participation.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Naming a leader without explaining why their knowledge mattered.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q2\"><h3>Question 2</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>2.\tReflect on what you have learned, including strengths and areas for growth as an equity-driven leader. Then discuss and refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3 as to how you addressed equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at your school.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Explain a leadership strength and growth need with evidence from the cycle.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> T1, Q1-Q3, G1, and F1.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>A strength was recognizing that declining schoolwide absence did not remove the persistent subgroup disparity. Comparing the successful and unsuccessful support cases also kept me from treating every family experience as identical. A growth need was my initial assumption that clearer reminders were enough. The interviews and feedback pushed me to consider time, language, accessibility, and whether a response reached the intended student. I strengthened a proposal; I have no implementation results demonstrating improved attendance.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> Reflection connects decisions, evidence, context, and limits.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Reporting that the gap closed when no strategy has been implemented.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section><section class=\"question\" id=\"d-q3\"><h3>Question 3</h3><div class=\"official\"><p class=\"eyebrow\">Official template prompt</p><p>3.\tBased on the analysis of your experiences in Cycle 1, identify specific professional learning goals for yourself and describe future steps for your professional growth.</p></div><p><b>What this asks:</b> Name a professional learning goal, actions, and evidence of your growth.</p><p><b>Evidence to have:</b> The limitation identified in D2.</p><blockquote><p class=\"eyebrow\">Fictional worked response</p><p>Over the next six weeks, I will practice checking the difference between offering support and establishing that it is accessible. In two planning meetings I will record the evidence considered, whose perspective is missing, an alternative explanation, and any decision changed by family or student input. A mentor will review both decision logs with me. I will evaluate whether those perspectives changed my reasoning and the proposal, while continuing to examine disability-specific attendance research.</p></blockquote><p><b>Why it works:</b> The goal is observable and grows directly from the reflection.</p><p><b>What weakens it:</b> Promising to \"be a better communicator\" without a practice or a way to examine it.</p><p class=\"source-note\">Read this response together with the related tables and explanations for <a href=\"#cycle/c1/rubric-1-8\">Rubric 1.8</a>.</p></section>\n<p>Read each complete descriptor with the teaching explanation. Levels 4 and 5 include the lower-level requirements specified by the official rubric. These examples do not predict a score.</p><section class=\"rubric\" id=\"rubric-1-8\"><h3>Rubric 1.8</h3><p>Essential Question: How did the leadership feedback shape the candidate\u2019s equity-focused strategy, build educational partner buy-in, and support their growth in addressing equity needs and gaps for the identified student group? How does the candidate reflect on their current strengths and identify future areas for growth in equity leadership?</p><div class=\"c1-table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Scrollable evidence table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Complete official descriptor</th><th scope=\"col\">Teaching explanation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">1</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate does not discuss or only mentions their selected administrator/supervisor/educational leader and/or the importance of educational partner buy-in is not discussed in the reflection. OR Candidate does not reflect on their growth as an equity-driven leader and/or does not address the equity needs or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site. OR Candidate does not refer to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. OR Candidate does not discuss strengths or areas for future professional growth.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Key reflection elements or evidence are absent; any listed OR condition matters.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">2</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate provides limited insight into why they selected the administrator/supervisor/educational leader for feedback and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in. Candidate vaguely reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and minimally discusses their ability to address the equity needs and/or equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate does not clearly explain their current strengths and/or vaguely mentions future areas for growth in equity leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">The feedback rationale, learning, or strengths and growth are vague.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">3</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">Candidate clearly explains their rationale for selecting the administrator/supervisor/educational leader to provide feedback on the selected strategy(ies) and how that feedback impacted their approach to build buy-in from school personnel. Candidate clearly reflects on their growth as an equity-driven leader and discusses their ability to address the equity needs and equity gaps for the identified group of students at the selected school site, referring to specific, relevant evidence from Steps 1, 2, and/or 3. Candidate clearly explains their current strengths and identifies future areas for growth in equity leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain feedback selection, effects on buy-in, and evidence-supported strengths and growth.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">4</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Level 3, plus: Candidate\u2019s reflection demonstrates how the school context\u2014 including social, economic, or cultural contexts\u2014 impacts their approach to providing equity-driven leadership.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Explain how social, economic, or cultural context shapes equity leadership.</td></tr><tr><td data-label=\"Level\">5</td><td data-label=\"Complete official descriptor\">All of Levels 3 &amp; 4, plus: Candidate utilizes experiences from Steps 1, 2, and 3 in confronting equity issues and gaps to describe what leadership skills have been learned and how those skills may be used in the future.</td><td data-label=\"Teaching explanation\">Use experiences from Steps 1-3 to explain learned leadership skills and future uses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Source of Evidence: \u2022 Part D: Reflective Narrative (up to 5 pages of written or up to 5 minutes of video explanation) CAPE Standard 5; Elements 5A, 5B CAPE Standard 6; Element 6A</p><p>CTC Version 09, printed page 21. <a href=\"sources/CalAPA_C1_AssessmentGuide.pdf#page=25\">Original rubric page</a>.</p><p class=\"c1-rubric-example\">In the fictional example: The reflection uses the numerical pattern, qualitative counterexamples, and leader feedback to explain a strength and growth need. Context and future use of learned skills need explanation, not just labels.</p></section>\n<section class=\"c1-check\"><h3>Before moving on</h3><ul><li>Does the reflection refer to specific evidence and decisions from the cycle?</li><li>Have you explained why you selected the feedback provider and how feedback affected buy-in?</li><li>Does the learning goal name a practice, timeframe, and way to examine your growth?</li></ul><p><a href=\"#unit/c1-reflect/walkthrough\">Additional graduation example: reflect</a> &middot; <a href=\"#cycle/c1\">Return to the Cycle 1 opening</a></p></section></section>"}]};
